1 NOTE DSI: He was mythic and historical founder of unified Egyptian empire. Horus name inscribed using two symbols: of catfish (nar) and chisel (mr). Meaning of the name is not clear, some scholars interpret it as The Striking Catfish, 
2 CONC others state the name should be read out as Mer-Nar and interpreted  Beloved of Nar . Menes is a Greek form of the name Meni which appears in documents as late as from dynasty XVIII (scarab of Hatshepsut and Totmes III). Interpret
2 CONC ation of the name Menes in also troublesome. Presumably it means:  The one who remains . It may have something to do with god Min and so is this name presented by  Herodotus. In J.P. Allen opinion this name is related to name of t
2 CONC he city of Memphis, founded by Menes. In a cylindrical sealing of early dynastic period, apart from the name of Narmer inscribed in serekh, there is also the symbol men which might identify Menes-Narmer. Some scholars (P. Kaplony,
2 CONC  W. Helck, D. Wildung) identify this king with Horus-Aha, the second ruler of this dynasty, and they suggest that he might have been a son of another Narmer. Significance of the king Narmer, as generally believed, was much higher 
2 CONC than of following rulers of dynasty I. Traces of Narmer s rule were found both in Egypt and abroad, in Nubia and Palestine (Rafiah, En Besor, Arad, Tel Erani). According to Manethonian tradition, the victorious king Menes ruled 30
2 CONC -62 years and met his end when carried off by hippopotamus. In  Herodotus  opinion, after he completed wide-scaled land drainage, the ruler founded city of Memphis. Also the first temple of a local deity Ptah, was erected. To the 
2 CONC times of Narmer are dated two important artifacts: votive palette and decorative mace-head, both found in Hierakonpolis. Both of them carry scenes referring to unification of the land, the event which, as generally accepted, had i
2 CONC ts beginnings yet long time before Narmer. Burial place   two-chambered tomb B17-B18 at the Umm el-Qaab necropolis at Abydos.
