1 NOTE DSI : The ruler usually identified with Menes (Narmer) and as such regarded as a first king and founder of the Dynasty I. His name is well known from numerous relics discovered at Abydos and Saqqara. Events taking place during the
2 CONC  rule of Hor Aha are known to us mainly from four annual plates. He made wars against Ethiopians and Libyans. He came from the Upper Egypt and married princess    Neithotep from the Lower Egypt. At Sais in the Delta he erected a t
2 CONC emple dedicated to Neith. According to W. Helck one of the plates records killing a human for sacrifice. In    Manetho s opinion the king was a practising medical who was writing treatises on anatomy. Although his chief wife was N
2 CONC eithotep, The king s wife was Neithotep, his son and successor was   Djer   was born from a concubine. Names of other Andjib s sons are known to us: Rechit, Het, Saiset, Imaib. To the time of king s Hor Aha rule are dated two big 
2 CONC sepulclar complexes at Naqada and Saqqara. A monumental mastaba, measuring 53 x 26 m at Nakada possibly belonged to the king or his wife. The tomb S3357 at Saqqara, previously ascribed to Aha, resembles a mastaba of 41,6 x 15,55 m
2 CONC  and 5 m high. Most likely he was buried in the three-chambered   tomb B10-B15-B19 in the Umm el-Qaab necropolis at Abydos.
