1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sneferu
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Sneferu, also spelled as Snefru or Snofru (in Greek known as Soris), was the founder of the Fourth dynasty of Egypt. Estimates of his reign vary, with for instance The Oxford History of Ancient Egypt suggesting a reign from around
2 CONC  2613 BC to 2589 BC,[4] a reign of 24 years while Rolf Krauss suggests a 30 year reign[5] and Stadelmann a 48 year reign. He built at least three famous pyramids that survive to this day and introduced major innovations in the des
2 CONC ign and construction of pyramids in Ancient Egypt.
2 CONT
2 CONT Snefru was the first king of the fourth dynasty of Ancient Egypt, who according to Manetho reigned for 24 years (2613-2589 BC).
2 CONT
2 CONT Manetho was an Egyptian priest, living in the third century BC, who categorized the pharaohs of dynastic Egypt into 31 dynasties.[6] Manetho’s schematic has its flaws, nevertheless, modern scholars conventionally follow his method
2 CONC  of grouping. The Papyrus Prisse, a Middle Kingdom source, supports the fact that King Huni was indeed Snefru’s predecessor. It states that “the majesty of the king of Upper and Lower Egypt, Huni, came to the landing place (i.e., 
2 CONC died), and the majesty of the king of Upper and Lower Egypt, Snefru, was raised up as a beneficent king in this entire land…”[7] Aside from Sneferu’s succession, we learn from this text that later generations considered him to be 
2 CONC a “beneficent” ruler. This idea may stem from the etymology of the king’s name, for it can be interpreted as the infinitive “to make beautiful.”[8] It is uncertain whether Huni was Sneferu’s father, however, the Cairo Annals Stone
2 CONC  denotes that his mother may have been a woman named Meresankh.[9] Hetepheres I is main Sneferu’s wife and the mother of Khufu,[10] the builder of the Great Pyramid on the Giza Plateau. Sneferu also had two other wives, and with t
2 CONC he first wife he had a son Nefermaat, who died before his father and so was Khufu the one who became a new pharaoh.
2 CONT
2 CONT The most well known monuments from Sneferu’s reign are the three pyramids he is considered to have built in Dahshur (the Bent Pyramid and the Red Pyramid) and Meidum (Meidum pyramid). Under Sneferu, there was a major evolution in 
2 CONC monumental pyramid structures, which would lead to Khufu’s Great Pyramid, which would be seen as the pinnacle of the Egyptian Old Kingdom’s majesty and splendour, and as one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World.
2 CONT
2 CONT The first of Sneferu’s massive undertakings is the Pyramid at Meidum. There is some debate among scholars as to Sneferu’s claim to the Meidum pyramid, and many credit its origin to King Huni. Nonetheless, the pyramid is a remarkab
2 CONC le example of the progression of technology and ideology surrounding the king’s burial site.
2 CONT
2 CONT The immense stone structure serves as physical testimony to the transition from the stepped pyramid structure to that of a “true” pyramid structure. Archaeological investigations of the pyramid show that it was first conceived as 
2 CONC a seven-stepped structure, built in a similar manner to the Djoser complex at Saqqara. Modifications later were made to add another platform, and at an even later stage limestone facing was added to create the smooth, angled finis
2 CONC h characteristic of a “true” pyramid.[23] Complete with a descending northern passage, two underground chambers, and a burial vault, the pyramid mainly follows the conventions of previous tombs in most aspects other than one: inst
2 CONC ead of being situated underneath the colossal structure, the burial chamber is built directly within the main body albeit very near ground level.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Bent Pyramid, also known as the Rhomboidal or Blunted Pyramid, attests to an even greater increase in architectural innovations. As the name suggests, the angle of the inclination changes from 55° to about 43° in the upper lev
2 CONC els of the pyramid. It is likely that the pyramid initially was not designed to be built this way, but was modified during construction due to unstable accretion layers. As a means of stabilising the monolith, the top layers were 
2 CONC laid horizontally, marking the abandonment of the step pyramid concept.[25] The internal components of the Rhomboidal pyramid have also evolved. There are two entrances, one from the north and another from the west. The subterrane
2 CONC an chambers are much larger, and distinguished by corbel walls and ceilings with more complex diagonal portcullis systems in place. J.P Lepre asserts:
2 CONT
2 CONT “It is apparent that with the interior design of the Bent Pyramid the
2 CONT
2 CONT architect was groping and experimenting, taking maximum
2 CONT
2 CONT advantage of the huge volume of the monument (50 million cubic
2 CONT
2 CONT feet), the largest pyramid constructed to that date.”
2 CONT
2 CONT The satellite pyramid complementing Sneferu’s Bent Pyramid introduces more change in the architecture of the time, when the passage way is built ascending westward (as opposed to the conventionally descending northward direction o
2 CONC f the passages of previously build pyramids) towards the burial chambers.[27]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Red Pyramid of Sneferu
2 CONT
2 CONT With the increase of innovation in Sneferu's building projects, one expects that his last pyramid, the Red Pyramid, will show the greatest complexity and change in architecture yet. Upon first glance, one may be disappointed seein
2 CONC g that the construction of the Red Pyramid seemingly is simpler than its predecessor. Lepre points out that some of the internal innovations that the previous pyramids boast seem to be missing in the king’s last monument. Although
2 CONC  the chambers and burial vaults are all present in the monument’s main body, no ascending passageway has been excavated, nor is there evidence of a western entrance or diagonal portcullis. Although the absence of these features ha
2 CONC ve dissuaded many archaeologists from further studying the Red Pyramid, Lepre is convinced that there are secret chambers waiting to be uncovered within the stone superstructure. Considering that the remains of King Sneferu have n
2 CONC ot yet been found, it still may be possible that his sarcophagus and mummy lie hidden in his mysterious last structure. Lepre claims: “the Red pyramid remains one of the chief pyramids that may possibly contain secret chambers, no
2 CONC t the least of which may be the true burial chamber of King Sneferu himself.” [28] Whether or not this conjecture is true needs to be left to modern archaeologists to determine.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sneferu’s architectural innovations served as a catalyst for later pyramid builders to thrive on. The first king of the fourth dynasty set a challenging precedent for his predecessors to match, and only Khufu’s Great Pyramid can r
2 CONC ival Sneferu’s accomplishments. As time progressed and ideology changed in Archaic Egypt, the monuments of the kings decreased greatly in size. The Pyramid of Menkaure being only a fraction of the size of the previous pyramids, th
2 CONC e focus of Egyptian ideology might have shifted from the worship of the king to the direct worship of the sun god, Ra.
2 CONT
2 CONT To enable Sneferu to undertake such massive building projects, he would have to secure an extensive store of resources. According to Guillemette Andreu, this is where the king’s foreign policy played a large part. Sneferu’s conque
2 CONC sts into Libya and Nubia served a twofold nature; the first goal was to establish an extensive labour force, and the second goal was to gain access to the raw materials and special products that were available in these countries.[
2 CONC 29] This is alluded to in the Palermo Stone:
2 CONT
2 CONT “[Reign of] Sneferu. Year ...
2 CONT
2 CONT The building of Tuataua ships of mer wood
2 CONT
2 CONT of a hundred capacity, and 60 royal boats of sixteen capacity.
2 CONT
2 CONT Raid in the Land of the Blacks, and the bringing in of seven thousand
2 CONT
2 CONT prisoners, men and women, and twenty thousand cattle, sheep, and
2 CONT
2 CONT goats…
2 CONT
2 CONT The bringing of forty ships of cedar wood (or perhaps "laden with cedar
2 CONT
2 CONT wood")…”
2 CONT
2 CONT Reused building materials found at the pyramid complex of Amenemhat I that are thought originally to have been a depiction of the Heb Sed for Sneferu - the left portion shows to the left, the pharaoh, before whom a figure is crouc
2 CONC hing and the central figure is a goddess; to the right are several figures in a procession - Metropolitan Museum
2 CONT
2 CONT According to this inscription, Sneferu was able to capture large numbers of people from other nations, make them his prisoners and then integrate them into his labour force. During his raids into Nubia and Libya, he also captured 
2 CONC their cattle for the sustenance of his massive labour force. Such incursions must have been incredibly devastating to the populations of the raided countries, and it is suggested that the campaigns into Nubia may have contributed 
2 CONC to the dissemination of the A group culture of that region. Sneferu's military efforts in Libya led to the capture of 11,000 prisoners and 13,100 head of cattle.[31] Aside from the extensive import of cedar (most likely from Leban
2 CONC on) described above, there is evidence of activity in the turquoise mines on the Sinai Peninsula.[32] There would also have been large-scale quarrying projects to provide Sneferu with the stone he needed for his pyramids.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sneferu's ancient cedar wood ship Praise of the Two Lands is the first reference recorded to a ship being referred to by name.
