1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Nyuserre Ini, also spelt as Neuserre Izi or Niuserre Isi and sometimes Nyuserra (in Greek known as Rathoris), was a Pharaoh of Egypt during the Fifth dynasty. He is frequently given a reign of 24 or 25 years[1] and
2 CONC  is dated from ca. 2445 BC to 2421 BC.[2] His prenomen, Niuserre, means "Possessed of Re's Power." Niuserre was the younger son of pharaoh Neferirkare Kakai by Queen Khentkaus II, and the brother of the short-lived king Neferefre.
2 CONC  [3]
2 CONT
2 CONT He is often thought to have succeeded his brother directly, but there is some evidence to suggest that Shepseskare reigned between the two, albeit only for a few weeks. Possibly, the latter had attempted to restore the lineage of 
2 CONC Sahure who might have been his father, deposing the lineage of Neferirkare Kakai in the process, but was unsuccessful.
2 CONT
2 CONT Niuserre's only known consort was Reptynub. A King's Daughter by the name of Khamerernebty (A) is thought to be the daughter of Niuserre. The identity of her mother is not known. Khamerernebty (A) was maried to the vizier Ptahshep
2 CONC ses.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Manetho's Epitome, Nyuserre is assigned a reign of 44 years but this data is considered suspect. The Turin King List data for his reign is badly damaged although scholars have usually assumed that it was 24 years. The Danish Eg
2 CONC yptologist Kim Ryholt who twice analysed the Turin King-list papyrus in the 1990s, however, notes that "Niuserre's reign is damaged. There is a distinct trace of a 10, 20 or 30, followed by a stroke after which the papyrus breaks 
2 CONC off. Accordingly, the possibilities are 11-14, 21-24, and 31-34 years [for Nyuserre], and not just 24 years" as is conventionally assumed.[5] However, since a Sed Feast scene is noted for Nyuserre from his solar temple at Abu Gura
2 CONC b, a reign of more than 30 years can be suggested for this Pharaoh.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Czech Egyptologist Miroslav Verner who has been excavating the Old Kingdom pyramids on behalf of the University of Prague in Egypt since 1976 concurs with the view that Nyuserre had a reign in excess of 30 years. He bases his 
2 CONC opinion here on this king's numerous building activities in Abusir which included the
2 CONT
2 CONT "construction of his own pyramid complex and two small complexes Lepsius no.XXIV and no.XXV for his wives,...the completion of the unfinished funerary monuments of his direct relatives Neferirkara, Khentkhaus II and Neferefra" as 
2 CONC well as the completion of this king's substantial sun temple building complex at Abu Gurab. "Beautiful reliefs with the scenes of the sed-festival from this sun temple are occasionally considered as indirect evidence of a long rei
2 CONC gn for this king. Generally, the historical authenticity...of such reliefs is doubted since the sed-festival scenes very probably belonged in the Old Kingdom to the standard 'Bildprogram' of the royal funerary monuments. However, 
2 CONC in Niuserre's case, the sed-festival scenes from Abu Ghurob [most probably reflect] the 30th jubilee of the king's ascension to the throne."
2 CONT
2 CONT The famous Double Statue in the Museum of Egyptian Art in Munich, portraying the pharaoh Nyuserre Ini as both a young man and an old man
2 CONT
2 CONT Nyuserre's burial place is a pyramid at Abusir located between those of pharaohs Sahure and Neferirkare Kakai. Its initial height was around 52m, with a base of about 79 square meters and a slope of 52 degrees. The volume of stone
2 CONC  was a total of about 112,000 cubic meters. It was originally covered with fine limestone as shown by some remaining casing stones. The burial chamber and antechamber were both lined with fine limestone as well and roofed with 3 t
2 CONC iers of megalithic limestone beams 10 meters long weighing 90 tons each.[7] His queen, Reput-Nebu, was also buried nearby. His magnificent temple of the solar cult at Abusir is called the Joy of Re. While military campaigns to Lib
2 CONC ya and Asia are mentioned in documents of this period, we have no specific evidence regarding the military activities of this ruler.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
