1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Ahmose Inhapy
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Jump to: navigation, search 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ahmose-Inhapy or Ahmose-Inhapi was a princess and queen of the late seventeenth dynasty of Egypt. She was a daughter of Pharaoh Tao I the Elder and was sister to Pharaoh Tao II the Brave, and the queens Ahhotep and Sitdjehuti. She
2 CONC  was married to her (half-)brother and bore him a daughter, Ahmose-Henuttamehu.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was mentioned in a copy of the Book of the Dead, owned by her daughter, and in the tomb of Amenemhat (TT53). A tomb was made for Inhapy in Thebes; her mummy was later reburied in DB320 where it was discovered in 1881 and is no
2 CONC w in the Egyptian Museum in Cairo.
2 CONT
2 CONT The mummy was found in the outer coffin of Lady Rai, the nurse of Inhapi's niece Queen Ahmose-Nefertari. It was unwrapped by Gaston Maspero on June 26, 1886 and was later examined by Grafton Elliot Smith who described Inhapi as a 
2 CONC strong-built, dark-skinned woman with a strong resemblance to her brother.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her titles were: King's Wife; King's Daughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Forrás / Source:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ahmose_Inhapy
