1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Ahmose-Meritamon
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Ahmose-Meritamun (or Ahmose-Merytamon; “Child of the Moon, Beloved of Amun”) was the royal daughter of Ahmose I and Ahmose Nefertari, and became the Great Royal Wife of her brother Amenhotep I, pharaoh of Ancient Egypt in the eigh
2 CONC teenth dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her remains were discovered at Deir el-Bahri, where she had been rewrapped and reburied by priests who had found her tomb that had been vandalized by robbers. It appears that she died when she was in her early thirties, with evide
2 CONC nce of being afflicted with arthritis and scoliosis.
2 CONT
2 CONT A limestone statue of this queen was discovered by Giovanni Belzoni while he was working in Karnak in 1817.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Her titles were: Great Royal Wife, King's Daughter, King's Sister, and God's Wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]See also
2 CONT
2 CONT Eighteenth dynasty of Egypt Family Tree
2 CONT
2 CONT Forrás / Source:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ahmose-Meritamon
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Ahmose-Sitamun
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia			
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Ahmose-Sitamun or Sitamun (“Child of the Moon, Daughter of Amun”) was a princess of the early Eighteenth dynasty of Egypt. She was the daughter of Pharaoh Ahmose I. A colossal statue of hers stood before the eighth pylon at Karnak
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT Her mummy was found in the Deir el-Bahari cache (DB320) and is today in Cairo, the Egyptian Museum.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her titles were: God's Wife; King's Daughter; King's Sister.
2 CONT
2 CONT Forrás / Source:
