1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nesitanebetashru
2 CONT
2 CONT Nesitanebetashru (ns-t3-nb.t-ỉšrw) was the name of two ancient Egyptian women. The name means “belonging to the lady of the ashru”; the ashru or isheru was a crescent-shaped sacred lake around the temples of solar goddesses, here 
2 CONC it refers to Mut.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nesitanebetashru of the 21st dynasty was a daughter of Pinedjem II, High Priest of Amun, and Neskhons. She is mentioned in the funerary text of her mother, written on a wooden tablet. Her mummy, coffins and ushabtis were found in 
2 CONC the tomb TT320 and are now in the Egyptian Museum in Cairo.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Nesitanebetashru of the 22nd dynasty was the wife of Pharaoh Sheshonk II and the mother of Pharaoh Harsiese A. She was also a Chantress of Amun. She is mentioned on a statue of Bes.[3]
