2 CONT Takelot IIFrom Wikipedia, the free encyclopediaJump to: navigation, search Takelot II  Pharaoh of Egypt  Predecessor Osorkon II  Successor Pedubast I  Royal titulary[show]Prenomen: Hedjkheperre Setepenre  
2 CONT
2 CONT Nomen: Takelot 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Consort(s) Karomama Merymut II 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hedjkheperre Setepenre Takelot II Si-Ese was a pharaoh of the Twenty-third Dynasty of Ancient Egypt in Middle and Upper Egypt. He has been identified as the High Priest of Amun Takelot F, son of the High Priest of Amun Nimlot C at
2 CONC  Thebes and, thus, the son of Nimlot C and grandson of king Osorkon II according to the latest academic research.[1] Based on two lunar dates belonging to Takelot II, this Upper Egyptian pharaoh is today believed to have ascended 
2 CONC to the throne of a divided Egypt in either 845 BC or 834 BC.[2] Most Egyptologists today including Aidan Dodson,[3] Gerard Broekman,[4] Jürgen von Beckerath,[5] M.A. Leahy and Karl Jansen-Winkeln also accept David Aston's hypothes
2 CONC is[6] that Shoshenq III was Osorkon II's actual successor at Tanis, rather than Takelot II. As Aidan Dodson and Dyan Hilton write in their comprehensive book on the Royal Families of Ancient Egypt:
2 CONT
2 CONT “ Takelot II is likely to have been identical with the High Priest Takelot F, who is stated in [the] Karnak inscriptions to have been a son of Nimlot C, and whose likely period of office falls neatly just before Takelot II's appea
2 CONC rance.[7] ” 
2 CONT
2 CONT Takelot II rather ruled a separate kingdom that embraced Middle and Upper Egypt, distinct from the Tanite Twenty-second Dynasty who only controlled Lower Egypt. Takelot F, the son and successor of the High Priest of Amun Nimlot C,
2 CONC  served for a period of time under Osorkon II as a High Priest of Amun before he proclaimed himself as king Takelot II in the final three regnal years of Osorkon II. This situation is attested by the relief scenes on the walls of 
2 CONC Temple J at Karnak which was dedicated by Takelot F – in his position as High Priest – to Osorkon II, who is depicted as the celebrant and king.[8] All the documents which mention Takelot II Si-Ese and his son, Osorkon B, originat
2 CONC e from either Middle or Upper Egypt (none from Lower Egypt) and a royal tomb at Tanis which named a king Hedjkheperre Setepenre Takelot along with a Year 9 stela from Bubastis are now recognised as belonging exclusively to Takelot
2 CONC  I. While both Takelot I and II used the same prenomen, Takelot II added the epithet Si-Ese ("Son of Isis") to his royal titulary both to affiliate himself with Thebes and to distinguish his name from Takelot I.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents [hide]  1 The Crown Prince Osorkon 2 Theban Uprising and Conflict 3 Marriages and children 4 References
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] The Crown Prince OsorkonTakelot II controlled Middle and Upper Egypt during the final 3 Years of Osorkon II and the first 2 decades of Shoshenq III. The majority of Egyptologists today concede that king Osorkon III was the 
2 CONC illustrious "Crown Prince and High Priest Osorkon B," son of Takelot II. A misunderstanding arose over his identity because in the Crown Prince's famous Chronicle, which was carved on the Bubastite Portal at Karnak, Osorkon dates 
2 CONC his actions by both the regnal years of Takelot II (years 11 through 24) —with a short year 25 left unmentioned — and then by those of the Tanite king, Shoshenq III (from regnal years 22 through 29).[9] While Kenneth Kitchen has i
2 CONC nterpreted this to mean that Shoshenq III succeeded Takelot II at Tanis, in fact Takelot II and Shoshenq III were likely close contemporaries because immediately after the death of his father in year 25 of Takelot II, Osorkon B st
2 CONC arted dating his activities to year 22, and not year 1, of Shoshenq III onwards. Consequently, there was never a two decade long break in Osorkon B's struggle to regain control of Thebes (from Year 1 to Year 22 of Sheshonq III) as
2 CONC  Kitchen's chronology implies because year 25 of Takelot II is equivalent to year 22 of Sheshonq III.[10] Osorkon B did not immediately ascend to his father's throne presumably because he was involved in a prolonged civil war with
2 CONC  his rival Pedubast I and, later, Shoshenq VI, for control of Thebes. Instead, he merely dated his activities to the serving Dynasty 22 Pharaoh at Tanis: Shoshenq III.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Crown Prince Osorkon B was not outmaneuvered to the throne of Tanis by Shoshenq III because both men ruled over separate kingdoms with the 22nd Dynasty controlling Lower Egypt, and Takelot II/Osorkon B ruling over most of Uppe
2 CONC r Egypt from Herakleopolis Magna to Thebes, where they are monumentally attested. In 1983, a donation stela was discovered by Japanese excavators (Heian Museum 1983) at Tehna which reveals that Osorkon III was once a High Priest o
2 CONC f Amun himself. This person can only be the well-known High Priest Osorkon B since no other Theban High Priests named Osorkon are known until the reign of Takelot III half a century later when the latter's son Osorkon F served in 
2 CONC this office.[11]
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Theban Uprising and ConflictIn Year 11 of Takelot II, an insurrection began under Pedubast I whose followers challenged this king's authority at Thebes. Takelot reacted by dispatching his son, Osorkon B, to sail southwards 
2 CONC to Thebes and quell the uprising. Osorkon B succeeded in retaining control of the city and then proclaimed himself as the new High Priest of Amun. Some of the rebel's bodies were deliberately burned by Osorkon to permanently deny 
2 CONC their souls any hope of an afterlife. However, just four years later, in year 15 of Takelot II, a second major revolt broke out and this time Osorkon B's forces were expelled from Thebes by Pedubast I. This caused a prolonged peri
2 CONC od of turmoil and instability in Upper Egypt as a prolonged struggle broke out between the competing factions of Takelot II/Osorkon B and Pedubast I/Shoshenq VI for control of Thebes. This conflict would last for 27 long years – f
2 CONC rom Year 15 to Year 25 of Takelot II and then from Year 22 to Year 39 of Shoshenq III when Osorkon B finally defeated his enemies and conquered this great city. Osorkon B proclaimed himself as king Osorkon III sometime after his v
2 CONC ictory.
2 CONT
2 CONT On other matters, the Chronicle of Prince Osorkon B, which is carved on the Bubastis Portal at Karnak, records Osorkon's activities between regnal years 11 and 24 of his father and then from regnal years 22 through 29 of Shoshenq 
2 CONC III. However, Takelot II's brief 25th year is attested by a donation stela made by his son in his position as High Priest at Thebes shortly before Takelot died; it granted 35 aurourae of land to Takelot II's daughter, Karomama E.[
2 CONC 12] Papyrus Berlin 3048 has also now been conclusively dated to Takelot II's (and not Takelot III's) reign due to the attestation of a certain Harsiese—designated the fourth prophet of Amun—in this document who is known to have se
2 CONC rved in office during king Takelot II's reign.[13] This papyrus contains several year dates including a year 13, year 14, year 16, year 23 and even a year 26—although a Year 26 date for Takelot II is unknown for this ruler and cou
2 CONC ld pertain to another pharaoh instead.[14] As of 2008[update], no tomb or final resting place has been found for this king.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Marriages and childrenTakelot II married his sister and Great Royal Wife Karomama Merymut II; they were the parents of:
2 CONT
2 CONT Osorkon B, the High Priest of Amun at Thebes who later became king Osorkon III. Bakenptah, general of Heracleopolis. Mentioned in a text dating to year 39 of Osorkon III and said to be a brother of the king.[15] Takelot II also ma
2 CONC rried a lady whose name was only partially preserved as Tashep[...]. They had a son:
2 CONT
2 CONT Nimlot, mentioned on a wooden stela (Turin 1468/Vatican 329) as the son of King Takelot and Tashep[...]. Thought to be a son of Takelot II.[15] Takelot also had a wife named Tabektenasket (I), they had a daughter:
2 CONT
2 CONT Isetweret (II). Married the Theban Vizier Nakhtefmut (C). Known from the coffins of her son Ankhpakhered and daughter Tabektenasket (II) (Berlin 20132 and 20136).[15][16] Other children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Djed-Ptah-ef-ankh, a younger son of Takelot II. Shebensopdet (II). Married the Fourth Prophet of Amun Djed-Khons-ef-ankh. She is known from a statue now in the Cairo Museum (CG 42211) [15][16] Karomama (E). Chantress of Amun.[15] 
2 CONC Possible further children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Tentsepeh (D) Married Ptah-udj-ankhef. Kitchen assumed she was a daughter of Takelot II. Bierbrier assumed she was a daughter of Osorkon II instead.[15][16] Ir-Bast-udja-tjau, married Pakhuru, the brother of Vizier Padiamonet. She
2 CONC  was a daughter of either Takelot II of Takelot III.[16] Di-Ese-nesyt, married Nespaqashuty B, the nephew of Vizier Padiamonet. She was a daughter of either Takelot II of Takelot III.[16] [edit] References Constructs such as ibid.
2 CONC , loc. cit. and idem are discouraged by Wikipedia's style guide for footnotes, as they are easily broken. Please improve this article by replacing them with named references (quick guide), or an abbreviated title. (February 2013) 
