2 TITL Hatshepsut: The Queen who became King | Ancient Origins
1 NOTE SOURCE NOTES:
2 CONT http://www.american-pictures.com/genealogy/persons/per04471.htm#0
1 NOTE RESEARCH NOTES:
2 CONT Princess/Queen of Egypt; Ruled with halfbrother, then alone 1479-1457 BCE
1 NOTE BIOGRAPHY: Scripture tells us that Moses was 80 years old at the time of the Exodus; if we use 1443 B.C.E. as the date of the Exodus, it would place Moses' birth in 1523. The throne princess (daughter of the previous pharaoh) at t
2 CONC hat time was named Hatshepsut. Hatshepsut was the chief wife of Thutmose II. They were childless. Hatshepsut, then, if we have dated events correctly, would have been the woman who adopted Moses and groomed him to be the next phar
2 CONC aoh. Is there anything in the Egyptian records to suggest that this is what might have happened?
2 CONT
2 CONT Thutmose II, Hatshepsut's husband, died while Moses was still young. Thutmose II had decreed that Hatshepsut should rule jointly with Thutmose III, his son by a secondary wife. Although Thutmose III was accorded the royal titles, 
2 CONC Hatshepsut effectively exercised the real power alone. Indeed, contrary to all precedent, she declared herself pharaoh. In keeping with this declaration, she had herself depicted in monumental statues as a man?with a masculine phy
2 CONC sique and even with the traditional false beard. All the traditional titles of pharaoh were appropriated by her, with only one exception--the mighty bull.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hatshepsut was eventually overthrown by Thutmose III about 1483--when Moses would have been about 40 years old! Was that a coincidence, or were the events related? Could the fact that Hatshepsut's designated successor was found to
2 CONC  be a murderer of an Egyptian (Exodus 2:14-15) have been the key to Thutmose III's success in winning the support of leading Egyptians in his power struggle with Hatshepsut? If this is the case, Moses would have had good reason to
2 CONC  flee Egypt.
2 CONT
2 CONT  -- Richard H. Paige
2 CONT     http://www.bibarch.com/Perspectives/7.2D.htm
