2 CONT
2 CONT Iset (queen)
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Iset (or Isis) was a queen of the eighteenth dynasty of Egypt. She was a secondary wife or concubine of Thutmose II.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Iset was the mother of Thutmose III, the only son of Thutmose II. Her son died in 1425 BC and her name is mentioned on his mummy bandages and a statue found in Karnak.[2].
2 CONT
2 CONT Although in these later instances Iset is referred to as Great Royal Wife, during the reign of Thutmose II the great royal wife was Hatshepsut. The great royal wife served in many official roles, including being the high priestess
2 CONC  in the temple. Thutmose II died in 1479 BC and, after his death, Hatshepsut was named as the next pharaoh and Thutmose III as co-regent. Thutmose III became the head of the armies of Hatshepsut.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hatshepsut ruled as pharaoh until her death in 1458 BC when her co-regent, Thutmose III, became pharaoh. At that time Iset received the title of "King's Mother" (since her son had become pharaoh) and she may then have been designa
2 CONC ted as a royal wife if she had not been previously when he was the co-regent. [3] At the time Thutmose III became pharaoh, his royal wife is thought to have become, Neferure, the daughter of Hatshepsut and Thutmose II, who had ser
2 CONC ved in this role throughout the reign of her mother as pharaoh. Neither Neferure nor their issue survived the death of Thutmose III, and a minor wife, Hatshepsut-Meryetre, became the mother of his successor.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is no time when Iset would have been designated as the great royal wife during her lifetime, but there were efforts toward the end of the reign of Thutmose III when he designated his son as co-regent for two years, and to a 
2 CONC much greater amount during the reign of his son as Amenhotep II, to rewrite history. Amenhotep II altered records and took credit for things Hatshepsut accomplished. Rewriting the history of his father, Thutmose III, in efforts to
2 CONC  eliminate Hatshepsut would have necessitated elevating his father's mother to the level of Hatshepsut's mother, hence the misrepresentation of Iset as the great royal wife of Thutmose II. The erasures and rewritings were not comp
2 CONC lete, however, and the accurate history was not lost.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is not certain whether Iset was a concubine or a secondary wife of Thutmose II. [2] She also received the title "God's Wife", but probably only retroactively, after her death. [2] Thutmose II would have been deified after his d
2 CONC eath, following the traditions of his time, so that would be the reason she was granted that title later.
2 CONT
2 CONT Forrás / Source:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iset_(queen)
2 CONT
2 CONT Iszet (királyné) [szerkesztés]
2 CONT
2 CONT Megtekintett lap  	(+/-)
2 CONT
2 CONT A Wikipédiából, a szabad enciklopédiából.
2 CONT
2 CONT Iszet
2 CONT
2 CONT hieroglifákkal
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Iszet (vagy Ízisz) királyné az ókori egyiptomi XVIII. dinasztia idején, III. Thotmesz anyja.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ő szülte a fáraó egyetlen fiát, a későbbi III. Thotmeszt, akinek múmiapólyáján és egy karnaki szobron említik Iszet nevét.[2] Bár van, ahol nagy királyi hitvesként hivatkoznak rá, férje életében biztosan nem ő, hanem Hatsepszut vi
2 CONC selte a címet, és Iszet nevéhez csak később, fia uralkodása alatt társították;[2] valójában még azt sem tudni, II. Thotmesznek a felesége vagy csak ágyasa volt.[3] Fia uralkodása alatt viselte továbbá „a király anyja” címet, valam
2 CONC int – bár feltehetőleg csak halála után – Thotmesz „az isten felesége” címet is anyjára ruházta.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Forrás / Source:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://hu.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iszet_(kir%C3%A1lyn%C3%A9)
2 CONT
2 CONT See also / Lásd még:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Family_tree_of_the_Eighteenth_dynasty_of_Egypt					
2 CONT
2 CONT See also / Lásd még:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=jdp-fam&id=I62330			
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT *http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isis
2 CONT
2 CONT *http://www.egyptartsite.com/isis.html
2 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT In the typical form of her myth, Isis was the first daughter of Geb, god of the Earth, and Nut, goddess of the Sky, and she was born on the fourth intercalary day. She married her brother, Osiris, and she conceived Horus with him.
2 CONT   Isis is often depicted as the mother of Horus, the hawk-headed god of war and protection (although in some traditions Horus's mother was Hathor). Isis is also known as protector of the dead and goddess of children
2 CONT ================================================================
