1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Satiah
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Satiah (also, Sitiah, Sitioh; “Daughter of the Moon”) was an Ancient Egyptian queen, the Great Royal Wife of Thutmose III[1].
2 CONT
2 CONT Her mother was the royal nurse Ipu[2]. It is possible that her father was the important official Ahmose Pen-Nekhebet[3]. Satiah died during her husband's reign and Thutmose's next Great Royal Wife was Merytre-Hatshepsut. No childr
2 CONC en of Satiah are known, though there is a possibility that Prince Amenemhat – Thutmose's eldest son, who died before his father – was her son[4].
2 CONT
2 CONT Satiah's titles include: King’s Wife (hmt-nisw), Great King’s Wife (hmt-niswt-wrt) and God’s Wife (hmt-ntr).[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Satiah is attested in:
2 CONT
2 CONT Abydos: The text on an offering table mentions her mother, the “nurse of the god” Ipu. The offering table was dedicated by the lector priest Therikiti. [6].
2 CONT
2 CONT Abydos: Bronze votive axe-head(?) (now in the Cairo Museum). The axe-head is inscribed with the name of Queen Sitiah [7].
2 CONT
2 CONT Temple of Monthu (Tod): Statue of Queen Sitiah dedicated by Tuthmosis III after her death (now in the Cairo Museum) [8].
2 CONT
2 CONT Pillar in the tomb of Tuthmosis III (KV34). Queen Sitiah is depicted behind Queen Merytre and Tuthmosis III. Behind Queen Sitiah we see the King's Wife Nebtu and the King's Daughter Nefertari. [9]
2 CONT
2 CONT Relief from Karnak. Satiah is depicted before Tuthmosis III. [10]
2 CONT
2 CONT A stela in the Cairo Museum. The stela shows Queen Satiah standing behind Tuthmosis III. [11]
2 CONT
2 CONT Forrás / Source:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Satiah
