1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Merytre-Hatshepsut
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT   (Redirected from Hatshepsut-Meryetre)
2 CONT
2 CONT Queen Merytre-Hatshepsut (or sometimes Hatshepsut-Meryet-Ra) was the principal wife of Pharaoh Thutmose III after the death of Satiah. She was the mother of Pharaoh Amenhotep II.[1] Of noble birth, she was the daughter of the Ador
2 CONC atrix Huy, whose statue in the British Museum (EA 1280) shows Huy holding a grandchild and represents the other children of Thutmose III and Merytre-Hatshepsut along the sides of her seated statue. She is not related to Queen Hats
2 CONC hepsut, the previous pharaoh of Egypt, though previously was often thought to be her daughter (Hatshepsut's only known child was Neferure).
2 CONT
2 CONT Merytre-Hatshepsut is known to have held the Titles: Hereditary Princess (iryt-p`t), Sole One, Great of Praises (wrt-hzwt-w’tit), King’s Mother (mwt-niswt), Lady of The Two Lands (nbt-t3wy), King’s Wife (hmt-nisw), Great King’s Wi
2 CONC fe (hmt-niswt-wrt), God’s Wife (hmt-ntr), God’s Hand (djrt-ntr)]].[2].
2 CONT
2 CONT Merytre-Hatshepsut is attested in:
2 CONT
2 CONT The temple of Tuthmosis III in Medinet Habu. The Queen is depicted standing behind a seated Tuthmosis III. She's depicted in full Queenly regalia, including the vulture cap, modius with double plumes and the fly-whisk. She is call
2 CONC ed "great royal wife".[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT The tomb of Tuthmosis III (KV43). On one of the pillars the queen, identified as Merytre, is one of three queens following Tuthmosis III. Merytre is followed by queen Satiah, Queen Nebtu and Princess Nefertari. [4]
2 CONT
2 CONT The tomb of Ra (TT72) in Thebes. Merytre Hatshepsut is depicted seated next to / behind her son Amenhotep II. [5]
2 CONT
2 CONT A stela (borne by the statue of a courtier).[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Merytre-Hatshepsut was originally meant to be interred in KV42, but was probably interred in KV35, with her son Amenhotep II.
2 CONT
2 CONT Forrás / Source:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hatshepsut-Meryetre
2 CONT
2 CONT Meritré-Hatsepszut [szerkesztés]
2 CONT
2 CONT Megtekintett lap  	(+/-)
2 CONT
2 CONT A Wikipédiából, a szabad enciklopédiából.
2 CONT
2 CONT Egyiptomi királyné
2 CONT
2 CONT XVIII. dinasztia
2 CONT
2 CONT Elődje:
2 CONT
2 CONT Szatiah
2 CONT
2 CONT Utódja:
2 CONT
2 CONT Tiaa
2 CONT
2 CONT Meritré-Hatsepszut
2 CONT
2 CONT Hatsepszut-Meritré, Meritré
2 CONT
2 CONT Dinasztia	XVIII. dinasztia
2 CONT
2 CONT Hieroglifa	
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Meritré-Hatsepszut
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Meritré
2 CONT
2 CONT Apja	ismeretlen
2 CONT
2 CONT Anyja	Hui
2 CONT
2 CONT Férje	III. Thotmesz
2 CONT
2 CONT Gyermekei	II. Amenhotep
2 CONT
2 CONT Menheperré
2 CONT
2 CONT Meritamon
2 CONT
2 CONT Meritamon
2 CONT
2 CONT Nebetjunet
2 CONT
2 CONT Iszet
2 CONT
2 CONT Sírja	KV42, KV35
2 CONT
2 CONT Meritré-Hatsepszut vagy Hatsepszut-Meritré, gyakan csak Meritré („Ré kedveltje, a hölgyek legnemesebbike”) ókori egyiptomi királyné a XVIII. dinasztia idején, III. Thotmesz nagy királyi hitvese, II. Amenhotep anyja.[1] Fia uralkod
2 CONC ása alatt is ő viselte a nagy királyi hitves címet, Amenhotep egy feleségét sem emelte erre a rangra (örökösének anyja, Tiaa nevét is csak fia uralkodása alatt említik úgy, mintha főfelesége lett volna férjének).[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ámon, Ré és Atum papnőjének, Huinak a lánya volt.[1] Korábban úgy tartották, Hatsepszut fáraónő kisebbik lánya, erről azonban már bebizonyosodott, hogy nem igaz,[3] bár lehetséges, hogy tagja volt a királyi családnak[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Thotmesznek a második főfelesége volt az első, Szatiah halála után (vitatott, hogy Nofruré, Hatsepszut lánya is Thotmesz felesége volt-e). Hat közös gyermekük ismert, II. Amenhotep fáraó, valamint Menheperré herceg, Nebetjunet, Me
2 CONC ritamon, Meritamon és Iszet hercegnők, akiket nagyanyjuk, Hui szobrán ábrázolnak. Iszet lehetett a legfiatalabb, mert alakját kisebbnek ábrázolják a többinél.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Meritré-Hatsepszut viselte az Isten felesége címet, ami a királyi család magas rangú nőtagjai között öröklődött; őutána lánya, Meritamon viselte.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Meritré-Hatsepszut még életben volt unokája, IV. Thotmesz uralkodása alatt, de ebben az időben számtalan ábrázolását átalakították IV. Thotmesz anyja, Tiaa számára.[1] A Királyok völgye 42. sír készült a számára, de nem itt temett
2 CONC ék el, hanem valószínűleg fia, II. Amenhotep sírjában KV35.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Forrás / Source:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://hu.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meritr%C3%A9-Hatsepszut
2 CONT
2 CONT See also / Lásd még:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=jdp-fam&id=I62325
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Menhet, Menwi and Merti
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Menhet, Menwi and Merti were three minor foreign-born wives of pharaoh Thutmose III who were buried in a lavishly furnished rock-cut tomb in Wady Gabbanat el-Qurud. Two of their names are West-Semitic in origin though none are Hur
2 CONC rian.[1] Each were given the title of King's Wife, and were likely only minor member of the royal harem.[2] It is not known if the women were even related since the faces on the lids of their canopic jars are all different.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Their tomb was originally discovered in August 1916 in the desert mountain cliffs in the southwest valley near Luxor and promptly looted by Qurnawi villagers.[4] When the Egyptian antiquities authorities tracked down the burial in
2 CONC  September of that year, all that was left at the original site were the objects discarded by the tomb robbers. It is thought that originally there were three intact burials at the site. Only the gold and stone objects had survive
2 CONC d as the wood and the mummies and wooden materials had disintegrated due to moisture "from water seeping through the cliffs above."[5] However, most of their surviving funerary remains were tracked down and purchased on the antiqu
2 CONC ities market at the time and many now reside at the Metropolitan Museum in New York.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Some of the treasures found within their tomb include gold diadems, gilded gold, toe and feet sandals, gold, carnelian and glass bracelets together with other vessels. Each of their bracelets are inscribed with the cartouche of Th
2 CONC utmose III. Other objects found in the tomb include Hathor decorated gold, silver and glass mirrors. While the collection is impressive, none of their headdresses employed the vulture motif used by more senior queens.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents [hide]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 The tomb of Thutmose III's foreign wives
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 Gallery
2 CONT
2 CONT 3 References
2 CONT
2 CONT 4 Literature
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]The tomb of Thutmose III's foreign wives
2 CONT
2 CONT Like the cliff tomb of Hatshepsut that Howard Carter found in Wady A, the tomb of Thutmose's foreign wives was also cut into a crevice.[8] Its entrance, though, "was cut into a platform about 10 metres from the wady floor, quite l
2 CONC ike Thutmosis III's tomb in the Valley of the Kings."[9] Menhet, Menwi and Merti's tomb consisted "of a single undecorated chamber--ca. 5 X 7.5 metres, by 1.5 to more than 2 metres high."[10]
2 CONT
2 CONT Forrás / Source:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Menhet,_Menwi_and_Merti
2 CONT
2 CONT Menhet, Menui és Merti [szerkesztés]
2 CONT
2 CONT Megtekintett lap  	(+/-)
2 CONT
2 CONT A Wikipédiából, a szabad enciklopédiából.
2 CONT
2 CONT Menhet, Menui és Merti három ókori egyiptomi királyné voltak, III. Thotmesz alacsonyabb rangú feleségei közé tartoztak. Feltehetőleg szíriai fejedelmek lányai voltak. Közös sírba temették el őket a Kubbanet el-Kurud vádiban. Nem t
2 CONC udni, rokonok voltak-e; arcuk a kanópuszedényeken nem hasonlít.[1] Kettejük neve nyugat-sémi, egyiküké sem hurri;[2] Merti neve a Márta név legkorábbi feljegyzett formája.[3] Mindhárman viselték az „a király felesége” címet, de va
2 CONC lószínűleg csak alacsonyabb rangú feleségek voltak.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT A sírt egy sziklamélyedésbe vágták,[5] de bejárata kb. 10 méterrel a vádi talaja fölött található, hasonlóan maga III. Thotmesz Királyok völgye-beli sírjáéhoz.[6] A sít egyetlen, díszítetlen helyiségből állt, melynek területe 5×7.
2 CONC 5 méter, magassága 1,5-től 2 m fölé terjed.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT A sírt 1916 augusztusában fedezték fel helyi lakosok a Luxortól délkeletre fekvő vádiban, és ki is fosztották,[7] az egyiptomi hatóságok szeptemberben már csak a sírrablók által eldobott tárgyakat találták a sírban. Eredetileg a h
2 CONC árom temetkezés valószínűleg érintetlenül vészelte át az évszázadokat, de csak a fém- és kőtárgyak maradtak meg, a faanyagokat és magukat a múmiákat megrongálta a fentről beszivárgó víz.[6] A legtöbb megmaradt tárgyat sikerült meg
2 CONC találni a korabeli régiségpiacon, és nagy részük ma a Metropolitan Szépművészeti Múzeumban található, New Yorkban.[8] A leletek közt találhatóak aranydiadémok, aranyozott saruk, arany-, karneol- és üvegkarperecek (mindegyik karper
2 CONC ecen III. Thotmesz kártusa szerepel), edények, Hathor képmásával díszített arany-, ezüst- és üvegtükrök. Egyik fejdíszen sem szerepel a keselyűmotívum, ami a magasabb rangú királynékat megillette.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Forrás / Source:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://hu.wikipedia.org/wiki/Menhet,_Menui_%C3%A9s_Merti
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Menhet, Menwi and Merti
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Menhet, Menwi and Merti were three minor foreign-born wives of pharaoh Thutmose III who were buried in a lavishly furnished rock-cut tomb in Wady Gabbanat el-Qurud. Two of their names are West-Semitic in origin though none are Hur
2 CONC rian.[1] Each were given the title of King's Wife, and were likely only minor member of the royal harem.[2] It is not known if the women were even related since the faces on the lids of their canopic jars are all different.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Their tomb was originally discovered in August 1916 in the desert mountain cliffs in the southwest valley near Luxor and promptly looted by Qurnawi villagers.[4] When the Egyptian antiquities authorities tracked down the burial in
2 CONC  September of that year, all that was left at the original site were the objects discarded by the tomb robbers. It is thought that originally there were three intact burials at the site. Only the gold and stone objects had survive
2 CONC d as the wood and the mummies and wooden materials had disintegrated due to moisture "from water seeping through the cliffs above."[5] However, most of their surviving funerary remains were tracked down and purchased on the antiqu
2 CONC ities market at the time and many now reside at the Metropolitan Museum in New York.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Some of the treasures found within their tomb include gold diadems, gilded gold, toe and feet sandals, gold, carnelian and glass bracelets together with other vessels. Each of their bracelets are inscribed with the cartouche of Th
2 CONC utmose III. Other objects found in the tomb include Hathor decorated gold, silver and glass mirrors. While the collection is impressive, none of their headdresses employed the vulture motif used by more senior queens.[7]
2 CONT
