2 CONT
2 CONT Thutmose (or, more accurately, Djhutmose) was the eldest son of pharaoh Amenhotep III and Queen Tiye, who lived during the Eighteenth dynasty of Egypt. His apparent death led to the reign of Akhenaten, his younger brother—as the s
2 CONC uccessor to the Egyptian throne—and the intrigues of the century leading up to Ramesses II, the start and ultimately the failure of Atenism, the Amarna letters, and the changing roles of kingdom's powers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince Thutmose served as a priest of Ptah in ancient Memphis.[1] His full royal titles are given in the sarcophagus of his pet cat: "Crown Prince, Overseer of the Priests of Upper and Lower Egypt, High Priest of Ptah in Memphis a
2 CONC nd Sm-priest (of Ptah)."[2] A small schist statuette of the prince as a miller is in the Louvre Museum "while a recumbent mummiform schist figure [of the Prince] is in Berlin."[3] Prince Thutmose is best remembered for the limesto
2 CONC ne sarcophagus of his cat, Ta- miu (she-cat), now in the Cairo Museum.[4] The schist statuette of Thutmose is inscribed on three sides with this text.
2 CONT
2 CONT "(right)...the king's son the sem-priest Djhutmose; (left) I am the servant of this noble god, his miller; (front) Incense for the Ennead of the western necropolis."[5] The cat sarcophagus of Prince Thutmose conclusively establish
2 CONC es that he was indeed the eldest son of Amenhotep III, since it provides his then current title of 'Crown Prince.'[2] Thutmose is also attested by a total of 7 pairs of calcite and pottery vases in the Louvre.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince Thutmose disappears from the public records and appears to have died some time during the third decade of Amenhotep III's kingship, fairly late.[6] In his place, his younger brother Amenhotep IV, better known as Akhenaten, 
2 CONC succeeded to the throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Freud's 20th century interpretation	Edit
2 CONT
2 CONT In his 1939 book Moses and Monotheism Sigmund Freud presented the idea that Crown Prince Thutmose's younger brother Akhenaten was associated with the Biblical character Moses. The idea was more recently (1998) reconsidered by Egyp
2 CONC tologist Jan Assmann in Moses the Egyptian.
2 CONT
2 CONT The schist recumbent bier	Edit
2 CONT
2 CONT The 10.5 centimetres (4 in) schist recumbent mummiform bier has Thutmose prone with the Ba, Soul Bird upon his lower breast. The sides of the small statuette contain the following hieroglyphs, recording him as 'S-M Thoth-MS-S ', "
2 CONC True of Voice" '–SM (priest?) Tutmosis, "True of Voice"-("deserving", worthy, or "venerable").[7] T3	I10 Z9	M23	G39	Z1
2 CONT
2 CONT S29	G17	G26	F31	S29	P8	P11 References	Edit
