1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Amenemhat III, also spelled Amenemhet III (c. 1860-1814 BC), was a pharaoh of the Twelfth Dynasty of Egypt. He ruled from ca.1860 BC to ca.1814 BC, the latest known date being found in a papyrus dated to Regnal Yea
2 CONC r 46, I Akhet 22 of his rule. He is regarded as the greatest monarch of the Middle Kingdom. He may have had a long coregency (of 20 years) with his father, Senusret III.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Statue from the Egyptian Collection of the Hermitage Museum
2 CONT
2 CONT Towards the end of his reign he instituted a coregency with his successor Amenemhet IV, as recorded in a now damaged rock inscription at Konosso in Nubia, which equates Year 1 of Amenemhet IV to either Year 46, 47 or 48 of his rei
2 CONC gn.[3] His daughter, Sobekneferu, later succeeded Amenemhat IV, as the last ruler of the 12th Dynasty. Amenemhat III's throne name, Nimaatre, means "Belonging to the Justice of Re."
2 CONT
2 CONT He built a first pyramid at Dahshur (the so-called "Black Pyramid") but there were building problems and this was abandoned.[4] Around Year 15 of his reign the king decided to build a new pyramid at Hawara.[5] The pyramid at Dahsh
2 CONC ur was used as burial ground for several royal women.
2 CONT
2 CONT His mortuary temple at Hawara (near the Fayum), is accompanied by a pyramid and was known to Herodotus and Diodorus Siculus as the "Labyrinth."[6] Strabo praised it as a wonder of the world. The king's pyramid at Hawara contained 
2 CONC some of the most complex security features of any found in Egypt and is perhaps the only one to come close to the sort of tricks Hollywood associates with such structures. Nevertheless, the king's burial was robbed in antiquity. H
2 CONC is daughter, Neferuptah, was buried in a separate pyramid (discovered in 1956) 2 km southwest of the king's.[7] The vizier Kheti was in office around year 29 under the king.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Rhind Mathematical Papyrus is thought to have been originally composed during Amenemhat's time.
2 CONT
2 CONT Other names:
2 CONT
2 CONT Ammenemes
2 CONT
2 CONT Lamares, Ameres (According to Manetho)
2 CONT
2 CONT Moeris
