1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Family
1 CONT
1 CONT Ahmose descended from the Theban Seventeenth Dynasty. His grandfather, Tao I and grandmother, Tetisheri, begat at least two children, Tao II and Ahhotep, who, according to the tradition of Egyptian kings, married and begat Kamose,
1 CONC  Ahmose, and several sisters.[5] Ahmose I followed in the tradition of his father and married several of his sisters, making Ahmose-Nefertari his chief wife.[6] They had several children including daughters Meretamun B, Sitamun A,
1 CONC  Siamun A, plus sons Ahmose-anhk, Amenhotep I, and Ramose A.[7] (The "A" and "B" designations after the names is a convention used by Egyptologists to distinguish between royal children and wives that otherwise have the same name)
1 CONC . They may also have been the parents of Mutneferet A, who would become the wife of later successor Thutmose I. He was succeeded by his eldest surviving son, who ruled as Amenhotep I.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Dates and length of reign
1 CONT
1 CONT Ahmose's reign can be fairly accurately dated using the Heliacal rise of Sirius in his successor's reign, but because of disputes over where the observation was made from, he has been assigned a reign from 1570-1546, 1660-1537, an
1 CONC d 1551-1527 by various sources.[8][9] Manetho gives Ahmose a reign of 25 Years and 4 Months.[10] This figure is supported by a Year 22 inscription from his reign at the stone quarries of Tura.[11] Examination of his mummy indicate
1 CONC s that he died when he was about thirty five, which also supports a 25-year reign, if he came to the throne at the age of 10.[12]
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Campaigns
1 CONT
1 CONT The conflict between the local kings of Thebes and the Hyksos king Apepi I had started sometime during the reign of Seqenenra Taa, (likely Ahmose I's father) and would be concluded, after almost 30 years of intermittent conflict a
1 CONC nd war, under the reign of Ahmose I. Seqenenra Taa was quite possibly killed in a battle against the Hyksos (a fact that his battered mummy gruesomely attests), and his successor Kamose (likely Ahmose's elder brother) is known to 
1 CONC have attacked and raided the lands around the Hyksos capital, Avaris (modern Tell el-Dab'a).[13] Kamose evidently had a short reign (his highest attested regnal year is year 3), and was succeeded by Ahmose I. The Hyksos king, Apep
1 CONC i I, is thought to have died at around the same time as Kamose and was suceeded by Apepi II Aqenienre.[14]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Ahmose ascended to the throne when he was still a child, and so his mother, Ahhotep, reigned as regent until he was of age. Judging by some of her rather unique descriptions of her regal roles while in power, including the general
1 CONC  honourific "carer for Egypt", she effectively consolidated the Theban power base in the years prior to Ahmose assuming full control. During Ahhotep's regency, Apepi II is thought to have remained bottled up in the delta, because 
1 CONC his name is not attested on any monuments or objects south of Bubastis.[15]
1 CONT
1 CONT Ahmose began the reconquest of the Hyksos starting in the 11th year of his reign. At about this time, Hyksos kingship changed once again from Apepi II to its final king, Khamudi.[16] According to the Kim Ryholt's analysis of the b
1 CONC rief military commentary on the verso of the Rhind Mathematical Papyrus, Ahmose had captured Heliopolis and Sile sometime around his 11th Regnal Year. This suggests that the Hyksos capital, Avaris was now besieged and would have f
1 CONC allen to Ahmose shortly after these events.[citation needed] Records of this campaign were discovered on the tomb walls of a soldier in it, Ahmose, son of Ebana. These records indicate that Ahmose I led three attacks against Avari
1 CONC s, the Hyksos capitol, but had to quell a small rebellion in Egypt. After this, In the fourth attack, he conquered the city.[17] He completely drove the Hyksos out by besieging Sharuhen in Gaza for three years, and conquering it b
1 CONC y the 16th year of his reign.[18]
1 CONT
1 CONT After defeating the Hyksos, Ahmose began campaigning in Syria and Nubia. His campaign in his 22nd year reached Djahy in Syria, and perhaps as far as the Euphrates. Details on this campaign are scarce because Ahmose, son of Ebana, 
1 CONC who was part of Ahmose I's navy, did not travel on this land expedition. Ahmose I's campaigns in Nubia are better documented. After a first campaign, a Nubian named Aata quickly rebelled against Ahmose, but was crushed. After this
1 CONC , an anti-Theban Egyptian man named Tetian gathered many rebels in Nubia, but was also defeated. Ahmose reestablished Egyptian rule over Nubia and placed it under a new administrative centre established at Buhen.[19] When reestabl
1 CONC ishing the national government Ahmose appears to have rewarded various local princes who supported his cause and that of his dynastic predecessors.[20]
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Art and monumental constructions
1 CONT A stelae bearing the likeness of pharaoh Ahmose I
1 CONT Enlarge
1 CONT A stelae bearing the likeness of pharaoh Ahmose I
1 CONT
1 CONT The defeat of the Hyksos occurred relatively late in Ahmose's reign, and his subsequent monumental building program likely only lasted no more than seven years[21], though much of what was started was likely finished by his son an
1 CONC d successor Amenhotep I.[22]
1 CONT
1 CONT With the re-unification of Upper and Lower Egypt under Ahmose came a rededication to the arts and monumental construction. Ahmose reportedly devoted a tenth of all the productive output towards the service of the traditional gods,
1 CONC [23] reviving massive monumental constructions as well as the arts.
1 CONT
1 CONT Excavations at the site of Avaris by Manfred Bietak have shown that Ahmose had a palace constructed on the site of the former Hyksos capital city's fortifications. Bietak found the Minoan-style fragmentary remains of the frescoes 
1 CONC that once covered the walls of this palace. There has subsequently been much speculation as to what role this Aegean civilization may have played in terms of trade and in the arts.[24].
1 CONT
1 CONT The art during of Ahmose I's reign harkened back the royal Theban style of the Middle Kingdom,[25] Stelae from this period were once more of the quality produced in the Middle Kingdom.[26] This reflects a perhaps natural conservat
1 CONC ive tendency to revive the fashion from the pre-Hyksos era. Despite this, it is interesting to note that only three statuary images of Ahmose I survive: a single shabti, presumably from his tomb (which has never been positively lo
1 CONC cated), and two life-size statues, one of which resides in the Brooklyn Museum of Art, the other in the Khartoum Museum.[27]
1 CONT The jewels and ceremonial weaponry found in with the burial of Queen Ahhotep, including an axe whose blade depicts Ahmose I striking down a Hyksos soldier, and the golden flies awarded to the Queen for her supportive role against 
1 CONC the Hyksos.
1 CONT Enlarge
1 CONT The jewels and ceremonial weaponry found in with the burial of Queen Ahhotep, including an axe whose blade depicts Ahmose I striking down a Hyksos soldier, and the golden flies awarded to the Queen for her supportive role against 
1 CONC the Hyksos.
1 CONT
1 CONT With the delta and Nubia under Egyptian control once more, Ahmose once again gained access to distant resources which were not present in Upper Egypt. He received gold and silver from Nubia, Lapis Lazuli from distant parts of cent
1 CONC ral Asia, and cedar from Byblos.[28] In the Sinai, he reopened the Serabit el-Khadim turquoise mines.[29] Also, although the exact nature of the relationship is uncertain, at least some Minoan designs have been found on objects fr
1 CONC om this period, and Egypt considered the Aegean to be part of its empire.[30] Ahmose also reopened the Tura limestone quarries to provide stone for monuments, alledgedly moving the stone by means of Asiatic cattle from phonecia, e
1 CONC ither taken from the Hyksos or from his syrian campaign.[31]
1 CONT
1 CONT Ahmose also resumed large construction projects like those before the second intermediate period. Ahmose began the building of temples, but he built mostly of brick and only in Upper Egypt and once in Buhen in Nubia. In Upper Egyp
1 CONC t, he built at the temple of Amun at Karnak, and at the temple of Monthu at Armant.[32] According to an inscription at Tura,[33] he used white limestone to build a Temple to Ptah and the southern harem of Amun, however he never fi
1 CONC nished either of those projects.[34] He also built a cenotaph for his grandmother, Queen Tetisheri, at Abydos.[35]
1 CONT
1 CONT He made Thebes, the capital of his ancestors the capital city for the whole of Egypt. This was also a strategic choice, as Thebes lays in the center of Egypt, and repelling any opposition would only entail at most traveling over h
1 CONC alf of the country to reach the land's boundaries.[36]
1 CONT
1 CONT Several stelae detailing the work done by him were found at Karnak, two of which depict him as a benefactor to the temple. One of these stelae, known as the "Tempest Stele", Ahmose claims to have rebuilt the pyramids of his predec
1 CONC essors at Thebes that were destroyed by a major storm[37]
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Pyramid
1 CONT
1 CONT The remains of his pyramid in Abydos were discovered in 1899, and identified as his in 1902.[38] This pyramid and the related structures were resurveyed in 1993 by an expedition sponsored by the Pennsylvania-Yale-Institute of Fine
1 CONC  Arts under the direction of Stephen Harvey.[39] This pyramid was the last pyramid ever built as part of a mortuary complex in Egypt. It has since lost its casing stones and the mound of sand upon which it was built collapsed, how
1 CONC ever when it stood, it was 52.5 m high with a relatively steep slope of about 60 degrees[39] (compare to the less acute 51 degrees of the Great Pyramid of Giza). The pyramid, or what remains of it, sits next to Ahmose's mortuary t
1 CONC emple, which, although also in ruins, contains thousands of inscribed fragments showing pictures of archers, ships, dead asiatics, and the first known representation of horses in Egypt.[39] There is some dispute as to if Ahmose wa
1 CONC s buried here, or if it was a cenotaph. Most pyramids in Upper Egypt have no mortuary temple attached and no rooms in or under them, so they are believed to be monuments to the king, but not his tomb. Because Ahmose's pyramid has 
1 CONC a mortuary temple, many believe it to be his actual tomb.[39] However, the pyramid is believed to have had no interior rooms, and has no rooms under it either, so other Egyptologists believe that it is in fact a cenotaph, and that
1 CONC  Ahmose was buried in Dra' Abu el-Naga' with the rest of the late 17th and early 18th Dynasties.[40]
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Mummy
1 CONT The mummified head of Ahmose I
1 CONT Enlarge
1 CONT The mummified head of Ahmose I
1 CONT
1 CONT Ahmose I's mummy was discovered in the Deir el-Bahri Cache above the Mortuary Temple of Hatshepsut, revealed in 1881. He was interred along with those of other 18th and 19th dynasty leaders Amenhotep I, Thutmose I, Thutmose II, Th
1 CONC utmose III, Ramesses I, Seti I, Ramesses II, and Ramesses IX, as well as the 21st dynasty pharaohs Pinedjem I, Pinedjem II, and Siamun.
1 CONT
1 CONT The mummy was unwrapped by Gaston Maspero on June 9, 1886. His mummy was found within a coffin that bore his name in hieroglyphs, and on his bandages his name was again written in hieratic script. He had evidently been moved from 
1 CONC his original burial place and was re-wrapped and placed within the cache at Deir el-Bahri during the reign of the 21st dynasty priest-king Pinedjum II, whose name also appeared on the mummy's wrappings. Around his neck had been pl
1 CONC aced a garland of delphinium flowers. The body bore signs of having been plundered by ancient grave-robbers, his head having been broken off from his body, and his nose smashed. [41]
1 CONT
1 CONT The body was 1.63 m in height. Ahmose I had a small face with no defining features, though he had slightly prominent front teeth, which may have been an inherited family trait, a feature also seen in some female mummies of the sam
1 CONC e family and his descendant Thutmose II.
1 CONT
1 CONT A short description of the mummy by Gaston Maspero sheds further light on familial resemblances:
1 CONT
1 CONT     ...he was of medium height, as his body when mummified measured only 5 feet 6 inches in length, but the development of the neck and chest indicates extraordinary strength. The head is small in proportion to the bust, the foreh
1 CONC ead low and narrow, the cheek-bones project, and the hair is thick and wavy. The face exactly resembles that of Tiûâcrai [ Tao II Seqenenre ], and the likeness alone would proclaim the affinity, even if we were ignorant of the clo
1 CONC se relationship which united these two Pharaohs.[42]
1 CONT
1 CONT Initial studies of the mummy were first thought to reveal a man in his 50s,[43] but subsequent examinations have shown that he was instead likely to have been in his mid-30s when he died.[44]
1 CONT
1 CONT His mummy now resides at the at the Egyptian Museum, Cairo.
1 CONT [edit]
