1 CONT ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Ahmose I, Founder of the 18th Dynasty and the New Kingdom
1 CONT of Ancient Egypt
1 CONT by Richard Warner 
1 CONT
1 CONT Egypt's 18th Dynasty that established the New Kingdom is, to most people interested in Egypt, a dynasty of stars.  It is the dynasty of Tutankhamun who was a fairly minor king, but perhaps the best known of any of the pharaohs.  I
1 CONC t was also the dynasty of the well known Akhenaten, and of Queen Hatshepsut.  
1 CONT
1 CONT The founder of this Dynasty is less well known to the general public, but unquestionably of major importance to Egyptian history.  He was Ahmose I, during who's reign Egypt was finally and completely liberated from the Hyksos. Var
1 CONC ious scholars attribute different dates to his reign, but he probably became ruler of Egypt around 1550 BC  at the age of 10, and ruled for a period of around 25 years before his death (examination of his well preserved mummy sugg
1 CONC est he was about 35 when he died).
1 CONT
1 CONT Ahmose I (Amosis to the Greeks) was given the birth name Ah-mose (The Moon is Born).  His thrown name was Neb-pehty-re (The Lord of Strength is Re).  He was probably a boy when he assumed the thrown, having lost his father Seqenen
1 CONC re Taa II and his brother Kahmose within three years of each other.  His mother was Queen Ashotep, a powerful woman who was perhaps his co-regent during his early years.
1 CONT
1 CONT Egyptologists believe that during his very early reign, little was probably accomplished and perhaps the Hyksos may have even gained some ground, recapturing Heliopolis.  However, by the end of his first decade in power, we know f
1 CONC rom an Autobiography of Ahmose, son of Ibana, a naval officer from El-Kab, that he laid siege on Avaris (The tomb of Ahmose Pennekheb, another soldier also records the campaigns). This was a long battle interrupted by the need to 
1 CONC put down insurrections in already liberated territories, but appears to have been successful sometime between his 12th and 15th year as ruler. Afterwards, he attacked the southwest Palestinian fortress of Sharuhen in a six year si
1 CONC ege that would finally put an end to Hyksos control of Egypt.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT A Stele of Ahmose I
1 CONT
1 CONT Next, he turned his attention to Nubia (Kush) and, while Kamose (his predecessor) may have gained some ground prior to his death, Ahmose I pushed the boundaries south to the Second Cataract.  Here, he established a new civil admin
1 CONC istration at Buhen probably initially headed by a Viceroy named Djehuty.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Ahmose I's Battle Ax
1 CONT
1 CONT Apparently, while Ahmose I was in Nubia, former Hyksos allies again attempted a few uprising in the north lead by an arch enemy of Kamose named Teti-en.  In this instance, Ahmose I's mother, Ahhotpe, was probably responsible for p
1 CONC utting down the rebellion and for this she was awarded the gold flies, an award for valor that was found on her mummy in her intact tomb at Thebes.
1 CONT
1 CONT After Ahmose I's campaigns in Nubia, he once again returned to Palestine during his 22nd year in power and may have fought his way as for as the Euphrates, according to information on a stela of Tuthmosis I.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Ahmose I married his sister, Ahmose-Nefertiri, who became Egypt's first great God's Wife of Amun, and had a number of children including: 
1 CONT
1 CONT Merytamun - eldest daughter of Ahmose-Nefertari (died young) 
1 CONT
1 CONT Tair - daughter of Kasmut 
1 CONT
1 CONT Satamun - 2nd daughter of Ahmose-Nefertari (died infant) 
1 CONT
1 CONT Sapair - eldest son of Ahmose-Nefertari (died young)  
1 CONT
1 CONT Saamen - 2nd son of Ahmose-Nefertari (died infant) 
1 CONT
1 CONT Aahotep - 3rd daughter of Ahmose-Nefertari (Queen) 
1 CONT
1 CONT Amenhotep I - 3rd son of Ahmose-Nefertari (King) 
1 CONT
1 CONT Satkames - 4th daughter of Ahmose-Nefertari (died aged ~30) 
1 CONT
1 CONT Henttameh- daughter of Thenthapi 
1 CONT
1 CONT Ahmose - daughter 
1 CONT
1 CONT We also know from Ahmose, son of Ibana that he supported his reign and rewarded local princes who had supported the Theban cause during the Second Intermediate Period by gifts of land (as recorded in Ahmose, son of Ibana's tomb at
1 CONC  el-Kab). We also know that he initiated some temple building projects, notably at Abydos.  However, though we know he reopened the Tura limestone quarries, little survives of his construction apart form a few additions to the tem
1 CONC ples of Amun and Montu at Karnak. However, a recent Dutch-Egyptian team of archaeologists believe they may have unearthed the remains of Ahmose's palace in the Al-Dabaa area in the Sharqiya Governorate of Egypt, a location that wa
1 CONC s probably the ancient Hyksos capital.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Pyramid of Ahmose I
1 CONT
1 CONT He was buried in the Dra Abu el-Naga area, but his tomb has yet to be found.  His actual mummy was found in the Deir el-Bahari cache.  He did have a cenotaph at South Abydos, consisting of a cliff temple and a pyramid and temple o
1 CONC n the edge of the Nile valley.  The pyramid which measures about 70 meters square is the last known royal example built in Egypt.  Some battle scene decorations within the pyramid may have depicted his wars with the Hyksos.  In th
1 CONC ese scenes are some of the earliest representation of horses in Egypt.
1 CONT
0 @N6000000002187988833@ NOTE Kamose
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Kamose was the last king of the Theban Seventeenth Dynasty. He was the son of Sekenenra Tao II and the brother of Ahmose, founder of the Eighteenth Dynasty. His reign fell at the end of the Second Intermediate Period; it is uncert
1 CONC ain when Kamose died, but reliable sources place his death around 1550 BC after a reign lasting more than 3 years. His reign is important for the decisive military initiatives he took against the Hyksos, who had come to rule much 
1 CONC of ancient Egypt.
1 CONT
1 CONT Over the years, the independent native princes in Thebes seem to have reached a practical modus vivendi with the later Hyksos rulers, which included transit rights through Hyksos-controlled Middle and Lower Egypt and pasturage rig
1 CONC hts in the fertile Delta. One text, Carnarvon Tablet I, found in Thebes, relates the misgivings of Kamose's council of advisors when the king proposed moving against the Hyksos, whom he claimed were a humiliating stain upon the ho
1 CONC ly fabric of Egypt. The councillors clearly did not wish to risk disturbing the status quo:
1 CONT
1 CONT     "...we are at ease in our (part of) Egypt. Elephantine [at the First Cataract] is strong, and the middle (of the land) is with us as far as Cusae [near modern Asyut]. The sleekest of their fields are plowed for us, and our cat
1 CONC tle are pastured in the Delta. Emmer is sent for our pigs. Our cattle have not been taken away....He holds the land of the Asiatics; we hold Egypt..."
1 CONT
1 CONT There is no evidence to support Pierre Montet's assertion that Kamose's move against the Hyksos was sponsored by the priesthood of Amun as an attack against the Seth-worshippers in the North (i.e., a religious motive for the war o
1 CONC f liberation). The Carnarvon Tablet does state that Kamose went north to attack the Hyksos by the command of Amun, but this is simple hyperbole common to virtually all royal inscriptions of Egyptian history, and should not be unde
1 CONC rstood as the specific command from this god. Kamose states his reasons for an attack on the Hyksos was nationalistic pride: in this same text he complains that he is hemmed in at Thebes between the Hyksos in the north and the Nub
1 CONC ians of Kush in the south, each holding "his slice of Egypt, dividing up the land with me...My wish is to save Egypt and to smite the Asiatics!"
1 CONT
1 CONT So it was that in his third year on the throne Kamose embarked and sailed north from Thebes at the head of his army. He surprised and overran the southernmost garrison of the Hyksos at Nefrusy, just north of Cusae, then led his ar
1 CONC my as far north as the neighborhood of Avaris itself. Though the city was not taken, the fields around it were devastated by the Thebans.
1 CONT
1 CONT A stela discovered at Thebes continues the account of the war from where the Carnarvon Tablet ends. His followers capture a courier bearing a message from the Hyksos king Aa-woser-ra Apopi at Avaris to his ally the ruler of Kush, 
1 CONC requesting his urgent support. Kamose promptly ordered a detachment of his troops to occupy the Bahriya Oasis in the Western Desert, which controlled the north-south desert route. Kamose, called "the Strong" in this text, then sai
1 CONC led back up the Nile to Thebes for a joyous victory celebration after what was probably not much more than a surprise spoiling raid in force which caught the Hyksos off guard.
1 CONT
1 CONT This Year 3 is the only one attested for Kamose, which may be the length of his reign. Donald Redford notes that Kamose's burial was very modest, in an ungilded coffin lacking even the royal uraeus.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Bibliography
1 CONT
