0 @N6000000002187608867@ NOTE Amenhotep I
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Djeserkare Amenhotep I (d. 1504 BC) was the second Pharaoh of the 18th dynasty of Egypt. He ruled from 1525 BC to 1504 BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT Amenhotep I was the third son of Ahmose and Ahmose-Nefertari. His elder brothers both preceded him in death clearing the way for his ascension to the throne in 1525 BC. Invaders from Libya took advantage of the death of Ahmose to 
1 CONC move into the western Nile Delta, prompting an immediate campaign for the new king. Despite Amenhotep's inexperience the invasion was quickly rebuffed.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to the tomb texts of Ahmose, son of Ebana, Amenhotep later sought to expand Egypt's border southward into Nubia and he lead an invasion force which defeated the Nubian response. Amenhotep commissioned the architect Ineni
1 CONC  to expand the Temple of Karnak, including a station for the barque of Amun. Amenhotep had one son, Amunemhat, with his Great Queen Ahhotep II; however, Amunemhat died when he was two. With no living heirs, Amenhotep was succeeded
1 CONC  by his military commander Thutmose I.
1 CONT
1 CONT Manetho states that Amenhotep I ruled Egypt for 20 Years and 9 Months. This data is confirmed by inscriptions from the tomb of a Magician named Amenemhet who specifically states that he served under Djeserkare Amenhotep I for 21 Y
1 CONC ears. There are two possible sites for the location of Amenhotep's tomb, one high up in the Valley of the Kings, KV39 and the other at Dra' Abu el-Naga', ANB. His body was found in the Deir el-Bahri Cache above the Mortuary Temple
1 CONC  of Hatshepsut and is now in the Egyptian Museum in Cairo.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT See also
1 CONT
1 CONT Amenhotep I with his wife.History of Ancient Egypt 
