1 CONT Amenhotep has fixed reignal dates. 22 July 1548 to 14 mar 1527. This is in part fixed by  several Rising of Sirus events recorded 9th yr 9th day of 3rd season. That had to be 1539 BC er CAH. Sirus (Sothis) is by far the brughtest 
1 CONC star in the sky and it just barely sets at Thebes for just a few days and then reappears. Because this is a fixed date in December and because the Egypitan calendar rotates in a 1460 year "sothic" cycle having even 365 days per ye
1 CONC ar; not the real 365.24219907 days. This makes this event shift in the Egyptian calendar gives us a precise date real subject only to the probable experimental error in the observaton i.e. + or - about 4 years. The orginal analysi
1 CONC s gave a 1548 date, but CAH corrected that since it was based upon a man standing with a sea horrizon and the priests at Thebes had -NOT a low "nautical" water horizon but a real land blocking part of the the sky horizon. This dat
1 CONC e associated with the below and known reignal day month years fix the reignal dates.
1 CONT
1 CONT Thuthmose III has a fixed reignal date, the start can be either 1490 or 1504 BC from a series of Lunar obsservations.  This is discussed at length by Lee W Casperson in JNES (Journal Near East Studies) p 139 -150 (following R A Pa
1 CONC rker's 1957 article) NES 16(1957)p39/43 and CAH. Casperson concludes as do I that 1504 is correct. But there is an even stronger argument in that by including the known Rising of Sirus dates from 1539 BC (CAH corrected from 1543 o
1 CONC riginal calculation) in 9th year 9th day third dseason of Amenhotep I and known reignal years months and days (DB Redford JNES 25(1966) p53 (but his dates are in error keyed to 1290 date of Ramses II, and totaly witout Sirus or Lu
1 CONC nar observations = add 14 years) the 1490 becomes impossible.  Thus we know the day and month of accession, and this gives us the year, 19 May 1504 BC.  Thuthmose III died day 30 of month VII thus we have another check fixed date.
