0 @N6000000002187604518@ NOTE Osorkon II
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Osorkon II was a pharaoh of the Twenty-second Dynasty Ancient Egypt between 872 BC to c.837 BC who ruled the country from Tanis, the capital of this Dynasty. Osorkon succeeded his father Takelot I to the throne of Egypt but was fa
1 CONC ced with the competing rule of his cousin, king Harsiese A, who controlled both Thebes and the Western Oasis of Egypt. Osorkon feared the serious challenge posed by Harsiese's kingship to his authority, but, when Harsiese convenie
1 CONC ntly died in 860 BC, Osorkon II ensured that this problem would not recur by appointing his own son Nimlot C as High Priest of Amun at Thebes. His younger son Shoshenq was made High Priest of Ptah at Memphis. In this period in Egy
1 CONC pt's history, priestly and political power were at their most inseparable.
1 CONT
1 CONT Despite his astuteness in dealings with matters at home, Osorkon was forced to be more aggressive on the international scene. The growing power of Assyria meant the latter's continued meddling in the affairs of Israel and Syria – 
1 CONC territories well within Egypt's sphere of influence. In 853 BC Osorkon's forces, in a coalition with those of Israel and Byblos, defeated the army of Shalmaneser III at the Battle of Qarqar thereby halting Assyrian expansion in Ca
1 CONC naan, albeit only briefly.
1 CONT
1 CONT Osorkon II devoted considerable resources into building projects – adding to the temple of Bastet at Bubastis which featured a large new hall decorated with scenes depicting his Sed festival and images of his Queen Karomama. Mutem
1 CONC hat was another of his wives. Monumental construction was also performed at Thebes, Memphis, Tanis and Leontopolis. Osorkon II built Temple J at Karnak during the final years of his reign, which was decorated by the then serving H
1 CONC igh Priest Takelot F(the future Takelot II), son of the High Priest Nimlot C. Osorkon II was one of the last great Twenty-second Dynasty kings of Tanis who ruled Egypt from the Delta to Upper Egypt as his successor, Shoshenq III l
1 CONC ost control of Middle and Upper Egypt in his 8th Year with the emergence of Pedubast I there. According to a JEA 81(1995) paper by Karl Jansen-Winkeln, Osorkon was served by the High Priest Harsiese B in his final 3 Years. Jansen-
1 CONC Winkeln also shows that both Harsiese A, and his son [..du] were only ordinary Priest of Amun, rather than High Priests of Amun, as was previously assumed; the inscription on the Koptos lid for [..du], Harsiese A's son, never once
1 CONC  gives him the title of High Priest. The statue CGC 42225, which mentions the High Priest Harsiese B and is dated under Osorkon II, was dedicated by the Letter Writer to Pharaoh Hor IX, who was one of the most powerful men in his 
1 CONC time(see D. Aston JEA 75(1989), p.152). However, Hor IX almost certainly lived during the final years of Osorkon II's reign since he features on Temple J in Karnak which was built late in this Pharaoh's reign, along with the servi
1 CONC ng High Priest Takelot F(son of the High Priest Nimlot C and future Takelot II). Hor IX later served under both Pedubast I and Shoshenq VI.(see p.152 of Aston's paper) This means that the High Priest Harsiese mentioned on statue C
1 CONC GC 42225 must be the second Harsiese – Harsiese B.
1 CONT
1 CONT Reign Length:
1 CONT
1 CONT Osorkon II died around 837 BC and is buried in Tomb NRT I at Tanis. He is now believed to have enjoyed a reign well in excess of 30 Years, rather than just 25 Years. The celebrations of his first Sed Jubilee were traditionally tho
1 CONC ught to have occurred in his 22nd Year but the Heb Sed date in his Great Temple of Bubastis is damaged and can be also be read as Year 30. Recently, it has been demonstrated that Quay Level Text No.14(dated to Year 29 of an Usimar
1 CONC e Setepenamun) belongs to Osorkon II on palaeographical grounds(see page 174-178 of G. Broekman's JEA 88(2002) paper on the Karnak Quay Texts). This finding suggests that Osorkon II likely did celebrate his first Heb Sed in his 30
1 CONC th Year as was traditionally the case with other Libyan era Pharaohs such as Shoshenq III and Shoshenq V. In addition, a Year 22 stela from his reign preserves no mention of any Heb Sed celebrations in this year as one would certa
1 CONC inly expect(see Von Beckerath) While Osorkon II's precise reign length is unknown, some Egyptologists such as Von Beckerath – in his 1997 book Chronology of the Egyptian Pharaohs – and David Aston have suggested a range of 38 to 4
1 CONC 0+ years but these figures cannot be verified.
1 CONT
1 CONT Osorkon II was succeeded by Shoshenq III at Tanis rather than Takelot II Si-Ese as was previously assumed because none of Takelot II's monuments have been found at Lower Egypt where other genuine Tanite kings such as Osorkon II, S
1 CONC hoshenq III and even the short-lived Pami(at 6-7 Years) are mentioned on donation stelas, temple walls and/or annal documents. The only documents which mention a king Takelot--such as a Royal Tomb at Tanis, a Year 9 donation stela
1 CONC  or a heart scarab featuring the nomen Takelot Meryamun--belong exclusively to Takelot I. Aidan Dodson, in his 1994 book, 'The Canopic Equipment of the Kings of Egypt,' observes that Shoshenq III built "a dividing wall, with a dou
1 CONC ble scene showing Osorkon II" and himself "each adoring an unnamed deity" in the antechamber of Osorkon II's tomb.(p.95) Dodson astutely notes that while one may argue that Shoshenq III erected the wall to hide Osorkon II's sarcop
1 CONC hagus, it made no sense for him to create such an elaborate relief if Takelot II had really intervened between him and Osorkon II at Tanis for 25 years unless Shoshenq III was Osorkon II's immediate successor and wished to associa
1 CONC te himself with the deceased king.(p.95) Hence, the case for establishing Takelot II as a Twenty-second Dynasty king and successor to Osorkon II is unproven.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Bibliography:
1 CONT Aidan Dodson, "The Canopic Equipment of the Kings of Egypt," (Kegan Paul Intl: 1994), p.95 
1 CONT David Aston, "Takeloth II: A King of the Theban 23rd Dynasty?," JEA 75(1989), pp.139-53 
1 CONT Gerard Broekman, "The Nile Level Records of the Twenty-Second and Twenty-Third Dynasties in Karnak," JEA 88(2002), pp.170-173 
1 CONT Karl Jansen-Winkeln, "Historische Probleme Der 3. Zwischenzeit," JEA 81(1995) pp.129-149 
0 @N6000000002187608878@ NOTE Sitamun
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Sitamun (also Sitamen, Satamun ) c. (1370 BC–unknown)
1 CONT
1 CONT Eldest daughter of Pharaoh Amenhotep III and his Great Royal Wife, Tiye. Sister of Pharaoh Amenhotep IV (Akhenaten). Granddaughter of Yuya and Thuya, although this is often debated, in favor of her being Amenhotep III's sister..
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Biography 
1 CONT 2 Titles 
1 CONT 3 Objects, Statues, Inscriptions 
1 CONT 4 Marriage to Amenhotep III: 
1 CONT 5 Significance 
1 CONT 6 References 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Biography
1 CONT Sitamun is very well attested in several contexts, most notably in the tomb of Yuya and Thuya where a series of three finely made chairs was discovered. As these chairs were used, and are of progressively larger size, it is assume
1 CONC d they belonged to Sitamun herself as she was growing up. They were then placed in her grandparents' tomb in the tradition of placing objects which had meaning in the deceased's life. Almost nothing is known of her life beyond bei
1 CONC ng the oldest daughter of a powerful (and long-lived) queen. Near the end of her father's reign (Year 31), she is elevated to the status of Great Royal Wife. She vanishes at the end of his reign and is not mentioned in the reign o
1 CONC f the next Pharaoh, Akhenaten.
1 CONT
1 CONT A separate chamber was carved for her in Amenhotep III's tomb in the Valley of the Kings, but there is no evidence she was ever buried there.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Titles
1 CONT Singer of the Lord of the Two Lands King’s Wife, King’s Great Wife, King’s Daughter, King’s Daughter Whom He Loves, Eldest Daughter of the King, Great Daughter of the King Whom He Loves. These identifications are problematic in th
1 CONC at Amenhotep III had a sister by the same name, and these titles could equally well apply to her. It was the tradition of Pharaohs of this period to marry their oldest sister (see: Thutmose IV/Iaret) even if that sister ultimately
1 CONC  was not the most important wife, that is, the mother of the heir. However, for the purposes of this entry Sitamun will refer to the daughter of Amenhotep III and Tiye only.
1 CONT
1 CONT In addition to the royal titles, Sitamun also bore the title "God's Wife of Amun" a title which is still not completely understood. It appears to have been hereditary in the 18th Dynasty, passed from the ruling queen to her eldest
1 CONC  daughter, who often became the next queen. It is worth noting that Hatshepsut held this position, prior to becoming Pharaoh. The position was not the mere empty formality that it became in the Ramessid Era (19th Dynasty). Its fun
1 CONC ction during the 18th Dynasty may have been to provide wives of royal blood to newly ascendant Pharaohs, effectively to legitimize their rule.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Objects, Statues, Inscriptions
1 CONT Sitamen's name appears on the following:
1 CONT
1 CONT Chairs in tomb of Yuya and Thuya
1 CONT
1 CONT Estate at Malkata
1 CONT
1 CONT Tomb of Amunhotep-son-of-Hapu (mentioned as manager of the estate)
1 CONT
1 CONT Statue of Amenhotep III/Tiye in Egyptian National Museum featuring several of their daughters, among them Sitamun (others: Isis, Nebetah).
1 CONT
1 CONT Relief from Amenhotep III's mortuary temple, found by William Matthew Flinders Petrie, currently in Petrie museum.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Marriage to Amenhotep III:
1 CONT Father/daughter incest among the ancient Egyptians is covered in other articles. Sufficient to note that the "marriage" of Amenhotep III to his daughter is often cited as an example. This marriage is almost without exception menti
1 CONC oned by authors dealing with the origins of the Amarna period (see: Alred, Reeves, Redford), and likewise almost never substantiated. The reasons given for this marriage are equally suspect. Authors propose that the marriage serve
1 CONC d to legitimize a ruler who at this point had occupied the throne of Egypt for thirty-one years.
1 CONT
1 CONT When evidence is presented, it consists of the following:
1 CONT
1 CONT - A blue-faiance kohl-tube with the cartouches of Amenhotep III and Sitamun
1 CONT
1 CONT - An alabaster bowl found at Amarna with the same cartouches.
1 CONT
1 CONT - The fact that near the end of Amenhotep III's reign, Sitamun is elevated to Great Royal Wife.
1 CONT
1 CONT It should be noted that Amenhotep III's son, Akhenaten (Amenhotep IV), likewise "married" his eldest daughter Meriaten, as well as his third daughter, Ankenspaaten, both by Nefertiti. However, both are also clearly identified as b
1 CONC eing the wives of the shadowy successor to Akhenaten, Smenkhare, and the famous Tutankamen.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sitamun is often proposed (on no evidence whatsover) as the mother of both Smenkhare and Tutankamen.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Significance
1 CONT Sitamun is among a handful of figures that appear near the end of the reign of Amenhotep III, the others being her brother (Thutmose V, heir apparent, but predeceasing Amenhotep III), and the well-known Akhenaten and Nefertiti. Th
1 CONC is was an era of Egyptian history in which women assumed far more powerful roles than ever before. Hatshepsut is the obvious example, but there are others such as Ahmose-Nefertari and Sitamun's own mother, Tiye. Sitamun herself as
1 CONC  the eldest daughter of a powerful queen would have been prepared for a political role. Yet she never seems to have fulfilled it, despite having her own estate at Malkata and numerous titles. One possibility is that she suffered t
1 CONC he same fate as the later Meritaten; that is, to be married to an heir who never reached his full potential (such as Thutmose V or the ephemeral Smenkhare). Another is that she simply died or went into seclusion.
1 CONT
1 CONT As the granddaughter of Yuya and Thuya, Sitamun is a direct link to the family which would eventually usurp the Egyptian throne, in the form of their son, Ay the last Amarna King. Curiously, while Sitamun herself is not mentioned 
1 CONC at Amarna, her steward is, although under a different name: Huy. Under this name he became the Royal Treasurer of Akhenaten.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT While any book on the Amarna Period of Egyptian history will mention Sitamun, none go into any detail except (thanks to Segereh on the Egyptian Dreams board):
1 CONT
1 CONT H. Schäfer's "Amarna in Religion und Kunst", Leipzig 1931. 
1 CONT E. Riefstahl "Thebes in the Time of Amenhotep III", NY 1964. 
0 @N6000000002187608897@ NOTE Ahmose-Meritamon
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Ahmose-Meritamon (or Ahmose-Merytamun), the daughter of Ahmose I, became the Great Royal Wife of Amenhotep I, pharaoh of Egypt in the 18th Dynasty. Her remains were discovered at Deir el-Bahri, where she had been rewrapped and reb
1 CONC uried by priests that had found her tomb violated by robbers. It appeared that she had died when she was in her early thirties, with evidence of being affected by arthritis and scoliosis.
1 CONT
1 CONT A limestone statue of the queen was discovered by Giovanni Belzoni while he was working in Karnak in 1817.[1]
1 CONT Contents
