0 @N6000000002187604491@ NOTE Takelot II
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Hedjkheperre Setepenre Takelot II Si-Ese Meryamun was a Twenty-Third Dynasty pharaoh of Ancient Egypt in Middle and Upper Egypt between 840 BC and 815 BC. He was Takelot F, son of the High Priest of Amun at Thebes, Nimlot C, and t
1 CONC hus the grandson of Osorkon II. Most Egyptologists today including Aidan Dodson, Gerard Broekman, Von Beckerath, M.A. Leahy and Jansen-Winkeln(see references below) accept that Shoshenq III was Osorkon II's actual successor at Tan
1 CONC is, rather than Takelot II. Takelot II instead ruled a separate kingdom — Dynasty 23 — in Middle and Upper Egypt which was separate from the Tanite 22nd Dynasty. Nimlot C's successor at Thebes, his son Takelot F, served as the Hig
1 CONC h Priest of Amun for a while under Osorkon II before he proclaimed himself as king in the final 3 Years of Osorkon II's reign. This situation is attested by the walls of Temple J at Karnak which was dedicated by Takelot F--in his 
1 CONC position as High Priest--while Osorkon II is shown as the celebrant and king. All the documents which mention Takelot II Si-Ese and his son, Osorkon B, originate from either Middle or Upper Egypt(none from Lower Egypt) and a royal
1 CONC  tomb at Tanis which named a Hedjkheperre Setepenre Takelot along with a Year 9 stela from Bubastis are now recognised as belonging exclusively to Takelot I instead. While both Takelot I and II used the same royal name, Takelot II
1 CONC  added the epithet Si-Ese(or Son of Isis) to his royal titulary both to affiliate himself with Thebes and to distinguish his name from Takelot I.
1 CONT
1 CONT Takelot II controlled Middle and Upper Egypt during the final 3 Years of Osorkon II and the first 2 decades of Shoshenq III. Most Egyptologists today concede that the future Osorkon III is the illustrious Crown Prince and High Pri
1 CONC est Osorkon B, son of Takelot II. A misunderstanding arose around his identity because in the Crown Prince's famous chronicle, he dates events by both his father's Regnal Years and then by those of the Tanite king, Shoshenq III. W
1 CONC hile Kenneth Kitchen has taken this to mean that Shoshenq III succeeded Takelot II, in fact Takelot II and Shoshenq III were likely close contemporaries because, immediately after the death of his father in Year 25 of Takelot II, 
1 CONC Osorkon B begins dating his activities from Year 22 of Sheshonq III onwards. Consequently, there was never a 22 Year break in Osorkon B's struggle to regain control of Thebes (from Year 1 to Year 22 of Sheshonq III) as Kitchen's T
1 CONC IPE Chronology implies because Year 25 of Takelot II is equivalent to Year 22 of Sheshonq III. Osorkon B did not immediately ascend to his father's throne presumably because he was involved in a prolonged civil war with his rival,
1 CONC  Pedubast I and later Sheshonq VI, for control of Thebes. Instead, he dated his actions to the serving Dynasty 22 Pharaoh: Shoshenq III. The Crown Prince Osorkon B was not outmaneuvered to the throne of Tanis by Shoshenq III as wa
1 CONC s previously thought because both individuals ruled over separate kingdoms with the 22nd Dynasty controlling Lower Egypt, and Takelot II/Osorkon B holding Middle and Upper Egypt from Herakleopolis to Thebes, where they are atteste
1 CONC d. In recent years, a donation stela has been discovered at Tehna which reveals that Osorkon III was once a High Priest of Amun in his own right. This person can only be the well-known High Priest Osorkon B.
1 CONT
1 CONT In Year 11 of Takelot II, an insurrection began under Pedubast I whose followers challenged this king's authority at Thebes. Takelot reacted by dispatching his son, Osorkon B, to sail southwards to Thebes and quell the uprising. O
1 CONC sorkon B succeeded in retaining control of the city and then proclaimed himself as the new High Priest of Amun. Some of the rebel's bodies were deliberately burned by Osorkon B to permanently deny their souls any hope of an afterl
1 CONC ife. However, four years later, in Year 15 of Takelot, a second major revolt broke out and this time Osorkon B's forces were expelled from Thebes by Pedubast I. This caused a prolonged period of turmoil in Upper Egypt as a despera
1 CONC te struggle broke out between the factions of Takelot II/Osorkon B and Pedubast I/Shoshenq VI for control of Thebes which lasted for 27 long Years(from Year 15 to Year 25 of Takelot II and then from Year 22 to Year 39 of Sheshonq 
1 CONC III when Osorkon B finally triumphed over his enemies and conquered this great city.) Osorkon B proclaimed himself as king Osorkon III sometime after this victory. On other matters, the Chronicle of Prince Osorkon B, which is carv
1 CONC ed on the Bubastis Portal at Karnak, records Osorkon's activities between Year 11 to Year 24 of his father and then from Year 22 to 29 of Shoshenq III. However, Takelot II's brief 25th Year is attested by a donation stela made by 
1 CONC his son in his position as High Priest, at Thebes.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT David Aston, "Takeloth II: A King of the Twenty Third Dynasty?," JEA 75(1989) pp.139-153 
1 CONT Aidan Dodson, "A new King Shoshenq confirmed?" GM 137(1993), pp.53-58 
1 CONT Jürgen von Beckerath, Chronologie des Pharaonischen Ägypten, MÄS 46 (Philipp von Zabern, Mainz: 1997) 
1 CONT Gerard Broekman, "The Reign of Takeloth II: a Controversial Matter," GM 205(2005), pp.21-33 
1 CONT Karl Jansen-Winkeln, "Historische Probleme Der 3. Zwischenzeit," JEA 81(1995) pp.129-14 
0 @N6000000008630614455@ NOTE There was a Lunar Eclipse recorded in 14 Takelot which was 24 Feb 822 BC.
1 CONT This fixes the reign to 836 start date. JNES Vol 24 p 61 (1967)
