0 @N6000000002187608780@ NOTE Hatshepsut
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT
1 CONT A statue of Hatshepsut commissioned without the false beard. The ureaus, a symbol of Pharaonic power, has been chiseled off the Khat head cloth.Maatkare Hatshepsut or Hatchepsut (late 16th century BC – c. 1482 BC) was the fifth Ph
1 CONC araoh of the Eighteenth dynasty of Ancient Egypt. Hatshepsut is generally regarded by modern Egyptologists as one of the most successful pharaohs, ruling longer than any female ruler of an indigenous dynasty. She was one of the mo
1 CONC st prolific builders of Ancient Egypt, commissioning hundreds of construction projects throughout both Upper and Lower Egypt and under her reign Egypt's trade networks began to be rebuilt, after their disruption during the Hyksos 
1 CONC occupation of Egypt during the Second Intermediate Period. She is believed to have ruled from 1503 BC to 1482 BC. Josephus writes that she reigned 21 years and 9 months, while Africanus states her reign lasted 22 years; both of wh
1 CONC om were quoting Manetho. Hatshepsut is regarded variously as the earliest known queen regnant in history, as the first known female to take the title Pharaoh, and the first great woman in history, although all of these claims have
1 CONC  been contested.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Family and pre-pharaonic life
1 CONT She was the eldest daughter of Thutmose I and Queen Ahmose, both of whom were of fully royal blood. Thutmose I and Ahmose are known to have had only one other child, a daughter Akhbetneferu (Neferubity), who died in infancy. Thutm
1 CONC ose I had been married to Mutnofret before Ahmose, and produced several half-brothers to Hatshepsut: Wadjmose, Amenose, Thutmose II, and possibly Ramose, through that union. Both Wadjmose and Amenose were prepared to succeed their
1 CONC  father, but neither lived beyond adolescence. In childhood, Hatshepsut is believed to have been favored by the Temple of Karnak over her two brothers—a view promoted by her own propaganda. She apparently had a loving relationship
1 CONC  with both parents, having been granted the prestigious title of "God's Wife of Amun" before the death of her mother; traditionally the pharaoh's wife was the sole holder of the title and only passed it on to her daughter or step-
1 CONC daughter upon her death.
1 CONT
1 CONT Upon the death of her father in 1492 BC, she married Thutmose II and assumed the title of Great Royal Wife, although God's Wife would contiue to be her favorite title. Some egyptologists believe that in Ancient Egyptian society, f
1 CONC emales rather than males passed on royal blood[1] and that although Thutmose II was already the designated heir, since he was of half-royal blood he needed to strengthen his position by marrying Hatshepsut. Thutmose II ruled for t
1 CONC hirteen years, during which it has been traditionally believed that Hatshepsut exerted a strong influence over him.
1 CONT
1 CONT Thutmose II had two daughters with Hatshepsut, Nefrure and Meritre. Hatshepsut groomed the elder one, Nefrure, as crown prince, commissioning official portraits of her wearing the false beard and side lock of youth, but she also p
1 CONC assed the female title of God's Wife of Amun on to Nefrure when she ascended to the throne. Some scholars speculate that this is evidence of Hatshepsut's that Hatshepsut was gooming Nefrure for the throne, others that she was mere
1 CONC ly planning another Hatshepsut. Whatever her intentions were, they came to nothing as Nefrure did not live into adulthood. Meritre may have been married, in childhood, to her contemporary Thutmose III, the son and male heir of Thu
1 CONC tmose II by a lesser wife and sister named Isis, and may also have been the mother of Amenhotep II. Some Egyptologists dispute that Meritre was Hatshepsut's daughter, and that she was in fact Hatshepsut or was a royal or noble chi
1 CONC ld named in Hatshepsut's honor.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Her rule
1 CONT Upon Thutmose II's death, the throne passed to Thutmose III and Hatshepsut—as the boy king's aunt and stepmother—was selected to be regent until he came of age. At first it seemed that Hatshepsut was patterning herself after the p
1 CONC owerful female regents of Egypt's then recent history, but as Thutmose III approached his majority it became apparent that she had only one model in mind: Sobekneferu, the last monarch of the Twelfth dynasty, who ruled in her own 
1 CONC right. However, Hatshepsut took one step further than Sobekneferu and had herself crowned Pharaoh around 1473 BC, taking the throne name Maatkare.
1 CONT
1 CONT Hatshepsut surrounded herself by strong and loyal advisors, many of whom are still known today: Hapuseneb, the High Priest of Amun; Nehsi, a Nubian general; and her closest advisor, the royal steward Senemut. Because of the close 
1 CONC nature of Hatshepsut and Senemut's relationship, some Egyptologists have theorized that Hatshepsut and Senemut were lovers. Among the evidence they offer to support this claim is the fact that Hatshepsut allowed Senemut to place h
1 CONC is name and an image of himself behind one of the main doors in Djeser-Djeseru (a rare and unusual sharing of credit), that Senemut had two tombs constructed near Hatshepsut's tomb (this was, however, a standard privilege for clos
1 CONC e advisors), and the presence of graffiti in an unfinished tomb, used as a rest house by the workers of her mortuary temple, depicting a male and a hermaphrodite in pharaonic regalia engaging in an explicit sexual act. Although th
1 CONC e belief that Hatshepsut and Senemut were lovers is well known, it is highly contested among Egyptologists[2]; all that is agreed on is that the steward had ready access to the queen's ear.
1 CONT
1 CONT As Hatshepsut reestablished the trade networks that had been disrupted during the Hyksos occupation of Egypt (the Second Intermediate Period), the wealth of the 18th dynasty that has become so famous since the discovery of the bur
1 CONC ial of Tutankhamun began to be collected. She oversaw the preparations and funding for a mission, led by Nehsi, to the Land of Punt. The expedition set out in her name with five ships, each measuring seventy feet long, and with se
1 CONC veral sails; each ship accommodated 210 men, including sailors and thirty rowers. Many goods were bought in Punt, most notably myrrh, which is said to have been Hatshepsut's favorite fragrance. She had the expedition commemorated 
1 CONC in relief at Deir el-Bahri, which is famous for its unflattering depiction of the Queen of Punt.
1 CONT
1 CONT Although many Egyptologists have claimed that her foreign policy was mainly peaceful[3], there is evidence that she led successful military campaigns in Nubia, the Levant and Syria early in her career.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Builder pharaoh
1 CONT
1 CONT Djeser-Djeseru is the main building of Hatshepsut's mortuary temple complex at Deir el-Bahri. Designed by Senemut, the building is an example of perfect symmetry that predates the Parthenon.Hatshepsut was a builder pharaoh. As pha
1 CONC raoh she initiated building projects that were grander and more numerous than those of any of her New Kingdom predecessors. She employed two great architects: Ineni, who had worked for both her husband and father, and the royal st
1 CONC eward Senemut. During her reign so much statuary was produced that almost every major museum in the world has a collection of Hatshepsut statuary; for instance, the Hatshepsut Room in New York City's Metropolitan Museum of Art is 
1 CONC solely dedicated to these pieces. Like most pharaohs she had monuments constructed at the Temple of Karnak. She had twin obelisks, at the time the tallest in the world, erected at the entrance to the temple. One still stands today
1 CONC ; the other has since broken in two and toppled. Karnak's Red Chapel, or Chapelle Rouge, was intended as a barque shrine and may have originally stood between the two obelisks. She later ordered two more obelisks to be made to cel
1 CONC ebrate her sixteenth year as pharaoh. However, one of the obelisks broke while being made, causing a third to be made to replace it. The broken obelisk was left at its quarrying site in Aswan, where it still is today, and has prov
1 CONC en valuable in learning how obelisks were quarried[4].
1 CONT
1 CONT The masterpiece of her building projects was her mortuary temple complex at Deir el-Bahri. It was designed and implemented by Senemut on a site on the West Bank of the Nile close to the entrance to the Valley of the Kings. The foc
1 CONC al point was the Djeser-Djeseru or "the Sublime of Sublimes", a colonnaded structure of perfect harmony nearly one thousand years before the Parthenon. Djeser-Djeseru sits atop a series of terraces that once were graced with garde
1 CONC ns. Djeser-Djeseru is built into a cliff face that rises sharply above it. Djeser-Djeseru and the other buildings of the Deir el-Bahri complex are considered to be among the great buildings of the ancient world.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Official propaganda
1 CONT
1 CONT Carved sphinx with face of Hatshepsut, depicted with the false beard, another symbol of pharaonic power, in the Cairo Museum.Hatshepsut was an excellent propagandist, and while all ancient leaders used propaganda to legitimize the
1 CONC ir rule, she is one of the most known for it. Much of her propaganda had religious overtones supported by the priests at the Temple of Karnak.
1 CONT
1 CONT In Ancient Egypt, women had a higher status than they did elsewhere in the ancient world, including the court-protected right to own or inherit property. Yet having a female ruler in her own right was rare: only Khent-Kaues, Sobek
1 CONC nefru and possibly Nitocris preceded her as ruling in their own name. Pharaoh was an exclusively male title; at this point in Egyptian history there was no word for a Queen regnant, only one for Queen consort, and as such, the wom
1 CONC en who ruled before Hatshepsut used the Queen consort's title of "God's Wife of Amun". Hatshepsut is unique in that she was the first woman to take the title of King regnant or King in the absence of a word or title for Queen regn
1 CONC ant.
1 CONT
1 CONT Hatshepsut slowly assumed all of the regalia and symbols of the office of King: the Khat head cloth, topped with an uraeus, the false beard and shendyt kilt. Many existing statues show her in both a feminine and masculine form. Ho
1 CONC wever, after this period of transition ended, all depictions of her showed her in a masculine form, with all of the pharaonic regalia and with her breasts omitted. Her reasons for doing this are a topic of great debate in Egyptolo
1 CONC gy. The traditional explanation is that her motivation for wearing men's clothing was sexual. However, most modern scholars believe in a more recent theory: that by assuming the exclusively male symbols of pharaonic power, Hatshep
1 CONC sut was asserting her claim to be King or Queen regnant and not "King's Great Wife" or Queen consort. Even after assuming the male persona, Hatshepsut still described herself as a beautiful woman, often the most beautiful woman, a
1 CONC nd although she assumed almost all of her father's titles, she declined to take the title "The Strong Bull"[5].
1 CONT
1 CONT One the most famous pieces of her propaganda is a myth about her birth. In this myth, Amun goes to Ahmose in the form of Thutmose I and wakes her with pleasant odors. At this point Amun places the ankh, a symbol of life, to Ahmose
1 CONC 's nose, and Hatshepsut is conceived. Khnum, the god who forms the bodies of human children, is then instructed to create a body and ka, or corporal presence/life force, for Hatshepsut. Khnum and Heket, goddess of life and fertili
1 CONC ty, lead Ahmose along to a lion bed where she gives birth to Hatshepsut.
1 CONT
1 CONT To further strengthen her position; the Oracle of Amun proclaimed that it was the will of Amun that Hatshepsut be Pharaoh. She publicized Amun's support by having endorsement by Amun carved on her monuments, for example:
1 CONT
1 CONT Welcome my sweet daughter, my favorite, the King of Upper and Lower Egypt, Maatkare, Hatshepsut. Thou art the Pharaoh, taking possession of the Two Lands. 
1 CONT She also claimed that she was her father's intended heir and made her crown prince of Egypt. Most scholars see this as revisionism on Hatshepsut's part, but one of her most well known biographers, Evelyn Wells, takes her at her wo
1 CONC rd. Propaganda supporting her claim was commissioned on the walls of her mortuary temple:
1 CONT
1 CONT Then his majesty said to them: "This daughter of mine, Khnumetamun Hatshepsut—may she live!—I have appointed as my successor upon my throne... she shall direct the people in every sphere of the palace; it is she indeed who shall l
1 CONC ead you. Obey her words, unite yourselves at her command." The royal nobles, the dignitaries, and the leaders of the people heard this proclamation of the promotion of his daughter, the King of Upper and Lower Egypt, Maatkare—may 
1 CONC she live eternally! 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Death
1 CONT
1 CONT These two statues once resembled each other; please note that the symbols of pharaonic power: the Uraeus, Double Crown, and False beard have been stripped from the left image. Images portraying Hatshepsut as Pharaoh were destroyed
1 CONC , or vandalized within decades of her death.Hatshepsut died, either as she was approaching or just entering middle age, in early February 1482 BC or 1483 BC[6]; no record of her cause of death has survived, although both natural c
1 CONC auses and murder have been proposed. Her mummy is believed to be missing from the Deir el-Bahri Cache, and has never been officially identified. An unidentified female mummy—found with Hatshepsut's wet nurse Sitre In and with her 
1 CONC arms posed in the traditional burial style of pharaohs—has led to the theory that the unidentified mummy might be Hatshepsut[7].
1 CONT
1 CONT After her death, many of her monuments were defaced or destroyed. Replacing the names on older monuments with the name of the current ruler was a common practice of pharaohs, but in some cases this is thought to have been an act o
1 CONC f damnatio memoriae—condemning a person by erasing him or her from recorded existence[8]. Egyptologists have differing views on who defaced Hatshepsut's monuments and their possible motivations including resentment for the belief 
1 CONC that a female Pharaoh was against Maàt.
1 CONT
1 CONT The traditional belief is that Thutmose III was responsible, and view the act as revenge for being denied the throne for so long. However, researchers such as Charles Nims and Peter Dorman have examined these erasures and found th
1 CONC at those which can be dated were done after the forty-second year of Thutmose's reign, while Donald B. Redford suggests a more sympathetic and complex motivation: Thutmose's need to demonstrate his legitimacy. Redford notes that:
1 CONT
1 CONT Here and there, in the dark recesses of a shrine or tomb where no plebeian eye could see, the queen's cartouche and figure were left intact ... which never vulgar eye would again behold, still conveyed for the king the warmth and 
1 CONC awe of a divine presence.[9] 
1 CONT
1 CONT As with most pharaohs, Hatshepsut had a number of names. Her birth name, or nomen, was Hatshepsut, to which she suffixed the epithet Khenmetamun, and prefixed the praenomen, or throne name Maat-ka-re. Her names are written as show
1 CONC n in Egyptian hieroglyphs on the right; Maat-ka-re to the top and Hatshepsut to the bottom.
1 CONT
1 CONT Maat-ka-re means "Maàt is the ka-spirit of Ra" and Hatshepsut means "Foremost of distinguished women, Joined with Amun". Together they mean "Maàt is the ka-spirit of Ra, Foremost of distinguished women, Joined with Amun". After sh
1 CONC e ascended the throne she changed her name from the feminine Hatshepsut to the male Hatshepsu[10].
1 CONT
1 CONT The names are technically transliterated as m3‘t-k3-r‘ ?3t-špswt–hnmt-?mn.
1 CONT
1 CONT Hatshepsut and Hatchepsut are the most common spellings of her name, but Hapshepsut and Hat-shep-set are sometimes found.
1 CONT
1 CONT In popular culture
1 CONT
1 CONT Hatshepsut as depicted in the 2005 computer game Civilization IV.As the Feminist movement matured, prominent women from antiquity were sought out and their achievements became increasingly publicized. Hatshepsut went from being on
1 CONC e of the most obscure leaders of Egypt at the beginning of the 20th century to one of its most famous by the century's end. Biographies such as Hatshepsut by Evelyn Wells romanticized her as beautiful and pacifistic woman — "the f
1 CONC irst great woman in History". This was quite a contrast to the 19th-century view of Hatshepsut as a wicked step mother usurping the throne from Thutmose III.
1 CONT
1 CONT At least three authors have written historical fiction novels featuring Hatshepsut as the heroine, and the Lieutenant Bak series of mystery novels is set during her reign. In 1960 a small main belt asteroid discovered by Cornelis 
1 CONC Johannes van Houten, Ingrid van Houten-Groeneveld and Tom Gehrels was named 2436 Hatshepsut in her honor. There is a popular theory that states that Hatshepsut was the princess who found Moses floating in the Nile, which has been 
1 CONC largely discredited by Egyptologists and Biblical scholars.
1 CONT
1 CONT Hatshepsut is one of the AI leaders featured in the turn-based strategy computer game Sid Meier's Civilization IV, and her mortuary temple was reproduced in the computer game Serious Sam. To date no film has been made featuring Ha
1 CONC tshepsut, but a screenplay named Daughter of Ra has won awards, and is being lobbied for online. In the first decade of the 21st century Hatshepsut was one of the most popular and recognized leaders of Ancient Egypt.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT See also
1 CONT History of Ancient Egypt 
1 CONT Eighteenth dynasty of Egypt Family Tree 
1 CONT List of Pharaohs 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Thutmose II Pharaoh of Egypt
1 CONT Eighteenth Dynasty Succeeded by:
1 CONT Thutmose III 
1 CONT [edit]
