0 @N6000000002187604463@ NOTE Shoshenq III
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT King Usimare Setepenamun Shoshenq III ruled Egypt's 22nd Dynasty for 39 Years according to contemporary historical records. Two Apis Bulls were buried in Year 4 and Year 28 of his reign and he celebrated his Heb Sed Jubilee in his
1 CONC  Regnal Year 30. Little is known of the precise basis for his successful claim to the throne since he was not a son of Osorkon II and Shoshenq's parentage and family ties are unknown.
1 CONT
1 CONT From Shoshenq III's Regnal Year 8, his reign was marked by the loss of Egypt's political unity, with the appearance of Pedubast I at Thebes. Henceforth, the kings of the 22nd Dynasty only controlled Lower Egypt. The Theban High Pr
1 CONC iest Osorkon B(the future Osorkon III) did date his activities at Thebes and (Upper Egypt) to Shoshenq III's reign but this was solely for administrative reasons since Osorkon did not declare himself king after the death of his fa
1 CONC ther, Takelot II. On the basis of Osorkon B's well known Chronicle document, most Egyptologists today accept that Year 25 of Takelot II is equivalent to Year 22 of Shoshenq III. (see D. Aston, JEA 75(1989), pp.139-153)
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Family
1 CONT
1 CONT Shoshenq III had at least 4 sons–Bakennefi A, Pashedbast B, Pimay who was known as the 'Great Chief of the Ma, son of the Lord of the Two Lands, Shoshenq', and Takeloth C–but they all appear to have predeceased their father throug
1 CONC h his nearly 4 decades-long-rule. Shoshenq's third son, Pimay ('The Lion' in Egyptian), was once thought to be identical with king Pami ('The Cat'), but it is now believed that they are two different individuals, due to the separa
1 CONC te orthography and meaning of their names.
1 CONT
1 CONT Shoshenq III was buried in the looted Royal Tomb NRT V at Tanis.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Osorkon II Pharaoh of Egypt
1 CONT 837 – 798 BC
1 CONT Twenty-second dynasty of Egypt Succeeded by:
1 CONT Shoshenq IV
0 @N6000000002187988946@ NOTE Shoshenq IV
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Hedjkheperre Setepenre Shoshenq IV ruled Egypt's 22nd Dynasty between the reigns of Shoshenq III and Pami. This Pharaoh's existence was first argued by David Rohl but Aidan Dodson settled the issue in a seminal GM 137(1993) articl
1 CONC e. Dodson's GM 137 arguments for the existence of Shoshenq IV are now universally accepted by all Egyptologists, including J. Von Beckerath and Kenneth Kitchen–the latter in the preface to his 1996 3rd edition of the 'Third Interm
1 CONC ediate Period in Egypt.' (page xxvi) While Shoshenq IV shared the same prenomen as his illustrious ancestor Shoshenq I, he is distinguished from Shoshenq I by his use of an especially long nomen–Shoshenq Meryamun Si-Bast Netjerheq
1 CONC awaset/Netjerheqaon which featured both the Si-Bast and Netjerheqawaset/Netjerheqaon epithets. These two epithets were gradually employed by Dynasty 22 Pharaohs starting from the reign of Osorkon II. In contrast, Shoshenq I's nome
1 CONC n reads simply as "Shoshenq Meryamun" while neither Shoshenq I, Osorkon I nor Takelot I ever used any epithets beyond the standard 'Meryamun' (Beloved of Amun) form during their reigns. The British Egyptologist Aidan Dodson--in hi
1 CONC s 1994 book on the Canopic Equipment of the kings of Egypt--perceptively observes that when the Si-Bast epithet "appears during the dynasty of Osorkon II," it is rather infrequent while the Netjerheqawaset/Netjerheqaon epithet is 
1 CONC only exclusively attested "in the reigns of that monarch's successors"(p.93)--ie: Shoshenq III, Shoshenq IV, Pami and Shoshenq V. This means that Shoshenq IV was a late Tanite era king who ruled Egypt after Shoshenq III. His preno
1 CONC men is explicitly attested on a published Year 10 stela(St. Petersburg Hermitage 5630) which bears his unique long form titulary. This stela mentions the Chief of the Libu, Niumateped, who is also attested in office in Year 8 of S
1 CONC hoshenq V. Since the title 'Chief of the Libu' is only first documented in use from Year 31 of Shoshenq III onwards (Dodson, GM 137, p.53), this new king again can only have ruled after Shoshenq III.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Burial
1 CONT
1 CONT Like Shoshenq III, Shoshenq IV was not a son of his predecessor and the exact grounds for his claim to the throne are unknown. He ruled Egypt between Shoshenq III and Pami for either 12 or 13 Years based on different Egyptologists
1 CONC ' interpretation of his reign. According to Dodson, excavation work in the looted NRT V Tanite tomb of Shoshenq III revealed the presence of 2 sarcophagi: one inscribed for Usermaatre Setepenre Shoshenq III and the other being an 
1 CONC anonymous sarcophagus. The unmarked sarcophagus, however, "was clearly a secondary introduction" according to its position in the tomb.(Dodson, GM, p.54) In the tomb's debris, several fragments were found from one or two canopic j
1 CONC ars bearing the name "Hedjkheperre-Setpenre-meryamun-sibast-Netjerheqaon."(Dodson, GM, p.54) Since the epithet Netjerheqaon was never employed by the 22nd Dynasty kings until the reign of Shoshenq III, this constitutes clear evide
1 CONC nce that Shoshenq IV was buried in Shoshenq III's Tanite tomb and succeeded this king. It also demonstrates that the king buried here was certainly not Shoshenq I.
1 CONT
1 CONT NOTE: The original king Shoshenq IV in pre-1993 books and journal articles has been renamed Shoshenq VI by Egyptologists because he was a Theban king who is only attested by Upper Egyptian documents. He was never a king of the Tan
1 CONC ite 22nd Dynasty of Egypt.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Aidan Dodson, "A new King Shoshenq confirmed?" GM 137(1993), pp.53-58.
1 CONT     * Aidan Dodson, "The Canopic Equipment of the Kings of Egypt," (Kegan Paul Intl: 1994), p.93.
1 CONT     * J. Yoyotte, Melanges Maspero I:4 (Cairo:1961), pp.142-43 [Comment on the Year 10 Shoshenq Si-Bast stela].
1 CONT     * Great Chiefs of the Libu: Niumateped
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Shoshenq III Pharaoh of Egypt
1 CONT 798 – 785 BC
1 CONT Twenty-second dynasty of Egypt Succeeded by:
1 CONT Pami
0 @N6000000002187608750@ NOTE Hatshepsut-Meryetre
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Queen Meryetre-Hatshepsut (or sometimes Hatshepsut-Meryet-Ra) was the principal wife of Pharaoh Thutmose III, and mother of Pharaoh Amenhotep II. Of noble birth, she was the daughter of the Adoratrix Huy, whose statue in the Briti
1 CONC sh Museum (EA 1280) shows Huy holding a grandchild and represents the other children of Thutomose III and Merytre-Hatshepsut along the sides of her seated statue. She is not related to Queen Hatshepsut, later pharaoh of Egypt.
1 CONT
1 CONT Merytre-Hatshepsut was originally meant to be buried in KV42, but was probably buried in KV35, with her son Amenhotep II.
