0 @N6000000002187608628@ NOTE Thutmose IV
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Menkheperure Thutmose IV (d. 1391 BC; sometimes spelled Thutmosis) was the 8th Pharaoh of the 18th dynasty of Egypt. He ruled from c.1401 BC to 1391 BC and is ascribed a reign of 9 Years and 8 Months by Manetho. This number is aff
1 CONC irmed by a Year 8 stela from his reign in Nubia. Thutmose IV was once thought to have enjoyed a reign of 34-35 years but this figure is not substantiated by the small number of monuments which he left behind as compared to those o
1 CONC f his son Amenhotep III, and the complete absence of attested Year dates for him after his 8th Year.
1 CONT
1 CONT Thutmose was born to Amenhotep II and Tiy; little is known about his brief ten-year rule. He suppressed an uprising in Nubia in his Year 8 around 1393 BC and was referred to in stele as the Conqueror of Syria, but little else has 
1 CONC been pieced together of his military exploits. Thutmose established good relations with Mitanni and finally married a Mitannian princess after much negotiations.
1 CONT
1 CONT Like most Thutmosids he built on a large scale. He completed an obelisk started by Thutmose III, which at 32m (105 ft.) was the tallest ever erected in Egypt, at the Temple of Karnak.
1 CONT
1 CONT Thutmose's most celebrated accomplishment was the restoration of the Sphinx at Giza and subsequent commission of the Dream Stele. According to Thutmose's account on the Dream Stele, while out on a hunting trip he stopped to rest u
1 CONC nder the head of the Sphinx, which was buried up to the neck in sand. He soon fell asleep and had a dream in which the Sphinx told him that if he cleared away the sand and restored it he would become the next Pharaoh. After comple
1 CONC ting the restoration he placed a carved stone tablet, now known as the Dream Stele, between the two paws of the Sphinx.
1 CONT
1 CONT Some Egyptologists theorize that because Amenhotep II did not name Thutmose IV his co-ruler, he did not intend for him to be his successor and that the restoration of the Sphinx and text of the Dream Stele was meant to bestow legi
1 CONC timacy upon his unexpected kingship.
1 CONT
1 CONT Thutmose's names in Egyptian hieroglyphs are shown above right. The technical transliteration of this name is d?wty-ms, which could be realised as Djehutymes. However, his name is more often rendered Thutmose, Thutmoses, or Thutmo
1 CONC sis after the Greek spelling of the name. His name means "Thoth bore him". Upon his ascension to the throne, Thutmose took a praenomen. Transliterated mn-?prw-r?, and realised as Menkheperure, this name means "Establisher of the f
1 CONC orms of Ra".
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Burial
1 CONT
1 CONT Thutmose IV was buried in the Valley of the Kings, in tomb KV43, but his body was moved to the mummy cache in KV35, where it was discovered by Victor Loret in 1898. An examination of his body shows that he was very ill and had bee
1 CONC n wasting away for the final months of his life prior to his death.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT See also
1 CONT
1 CONT     * History of Ancient Egypt
1 CONT     * Eighteenth dynasty of Egypt Family Tree
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Amenhotep II Pharaoh of Egypt
1 CONT Eighteenth Dynasty Succeeded by:
1 CONT Amenhotep III
1 CONT
1 CONT ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT The Pharaoh Tuthmosis IV, who ruled during Egypt famous 18th Dynasty, is probably most famous for his "Dream Stele, that can still today be found between the paws of the great Sphinx at Giza. Dreams were important in ancient Egypt
1 CONC  and were considered to be divine predictions of the future.  In Tuthmosis IV's "Dream Stele", he tells us that, while out on a hunting trip, he fell asleep in the shadow of the Sphinx (or apparently, the shadow of the Sphinx's he
1 CONC ad, for the monument was apparently buried in sand at the time).  In the young prince's sleep, Re-Harakhte, the sun god embodied in the Sphinx, came to him in a dream and promised that if he would clear away the sand that engulfed
1 CONC  the monument, Tuthmosis would become king of Egypt.   
1 CONT
1 CONT Tuthmosis IV's Dream Stele
1 CONT
1 CONT In part, the stele reads:
1 CONT
1 CONT "Now the statue of the very great Khepri (the Great Sphix) restin in this place, great of fame, sacred of respect, the shade of Ra resting on him.  Memphis and every city on its two sides came to him, their arms in adoration to hi
1 CONC s face, bearing great offerings for his ka.  One of these days it happened that price Tuthmosis came travelling at the time of midday.  He rested in the shadow of the great god.  (Sleep and) dream (took possession of me) at the mo
1 CONC ment the sun was at zenith.  Then he found the majesty of this noble god speaking from his own mouth like a father speaks to his son, and saying, 'Look at me, observe me, my son Tuthmosis.  I am your father, Horemakhet-Khepri-Ra-A
1 CONC tum.  I shall give to you the kingship (upon the land before the living)...(Behold, my condition is like one in illness), all (my limbs being ruined).  The sand of the desert, upon which I used to be, (now) confronts me; and it is
1 CONC  in order to cause that you do what is in my heart that I have waited."
1 CONT
1 CONT Obviously, the prince carried out these instructions, and thus became the eighth ruler of the 18th Dynasty.
1 CONT
1 CONT Right: Tuthmosis IV and His Mother, Tiaa
1 CONT
1 CONT Tuthmosis IV's name means, "Born of the God Thoth".  His throne name was Men-kheperu-re, meaning "Everlasting are the Manifestations of Re".  We can also find references to him under the names of Thuthmose IV, Thutmosis IV, and Dj
1 CONC ehutymes IV.  He ruled Egypt between 1419 and 1386 BC. He was apparently the son of Amenhotep II by his wife, Tiaa, but Egyptologists speculate whether, because of the wording of the "Dream Stele", his claim on the Egyptian throne
1 CONC  was legitimate. In fact, other evidence supports this contention.  His father, Amenhotep II, never recognized Tuthmosis as a co-regent, or announced any intent for Thutmosis to succeed him.
1 CONT
1 CONT We know that Tuthmosis IV was probably married to Mutemwiya, who produced his heir to the throne, Amenhotep III, though he never acknowledged her as either a major or minor queen.  It is possible, though now doubted by some, that 
1 CONC she was the daughter of he Mitannian king, Artatama, who sent his daughter to the Egyptian court as part of a diplomatic exchange.  Other of his wives included Merytra, who we believe later changed her name to Tiaa (same as his mo
1 CONC ther's name) and a non-royal wife, Nefertiry. He probably also married one of his sisters named Iaret. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Tuthmosis IV is not the best documented of Egyptian pharaohs.  We actually know very little about him in comparison to others of this dynasty.  Little military action appears to have occurred during his reign, although our knowled
1 CONC ge may be marred by the lack of texts.  We do know that there was a Nubian campaign in Year 8 of his rule, and that apparently there were also campaigns in Syria.  However, even though the king is referred to twice as the "conquer
1 CONC or of Syria", these may have actually been little more then policing actions, rather than full scale battles.  
1 CONT
1 CONT Little is also known of his building work.  Tuthmosis IV did finish a giant obelisk that was originally quarried at Aswan under Tuthmosis III, his grandfather. At 32 meters (105 feet) it was the tallest Egyptian obelisk that we kn
1 CONC ow of, and was uniquely intended to stand as a single obelisk at the Temple of Karnak.  Most of the obelisks were usually erected in pairs. However, Tuthmosis III originally intended for there to be a pair of these Obelisks.  Its 
1 CONC counterpart developed a fault during the quarry process, and remains today joined to the bed-rock at Aswan.  Today, the finished obelisk stands outside St. John Leteran in Rome, rather then in Egypt.
1 CONT
1 CONT He also began work work at most of Egypt's major temple sites and four sites in Nubia, but almost all of this was simply adding to existing monuments. Most of his work was adding to the temples of his father and grandfather, and p
1 CONC erhaps suggesting new sites and monuments to his son.
1 CONT
1 CONT We know of his minor building projects in the following locations:
1 CONT
1 CONT The Delta at Alexandria 
1 CONT Seriakus 
1 CONT Heliopolis (possibly) 
1 CONT Giza 
1 CONT Abusir 
1 CONT Saqqara 
1 CONT Memphis 
1 CONT Crocodilopos in the Fayoum 
1 CONT Hermopolis 
1 CONT Amarna 
1 CONT Abydos (a chapel) 
1 CONT Dendera 
1 CONT Medamud 
1 CONT Karnak 
1 CONT Luxor 
1 CONT The West Bank at Luxor (his tomb and mortuary temple) 
1 CONT Armant 
1 CONT Tod 
1 CONT Elkab 
1 CONT Edfu 
1 CONT Elephantine 
1 CONT Konosso 
1 CONT In Nubia at the following locations:
1 CONT
1 CONT Faras 
1 CONT Buhen 
1 CONT Amada (where he decorated the peristyle court) 
1 CONT Tabo 
1 CONT Gebel Barkal (a foundation deposit) 
1 CONT He also provided some decorations in the Hathor temple at the Serabit el-Khadim turquoise mines in the Sinai.
1 CONT
1 CONT His best attested building project we have available today is his own tomb, KV 43, located in the Valley of the Kings and discovered by Howard Carter. However, his mummy was missing from his  tomb, having been found five years ear
1 CONC lier in a cache of mummies located in the tomb of Amenhotep II.  Perhaps better known are the fine private tombs built by his nobles on the West Bank at Luxor (ancient Thebes) in an area commonly referred to as the Tombs of the No
1 CONC bles.  These include such notable tombs as that of Nakht (TT 52) and Menna (TT 69).
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT From his tomb, the Deceased Tuthmosis IV Receiving 
1 CONT the Gift of Life from the Goddess, Hathor
1 CONT
1 CONT References:
1 CONT
1 CONT Title Author Date Publisher Reference Number 
1 CONT Chronicle of the Pharaohs (The Reign-By-Reign Record of the Rulers and Dynasties of Ancient Egypt) Clayton, Peter A. 1994 Thames and Hudson Ltd ISBN 0-500-05074-0 
1 CONT Complete Valley of the Kings, The (Tombs and Treasures of Egypt's Greatest Pharaohs) Reeves, Nicholas; Wilkinson, Richard H. 1966 Thames and Hudson Ltd IBSN 0-500-05080-5 
1 CONT Monarchs of the Nile Dodson, Aidan 1995 Rubicon Press ISBN 0-948695-20-x 
1 CONT Oxford History of Ancient Egypt, The Shaw, Ian 2000 Oxford University Press ISBN 0-19-815034-2 
1 CONT Who Were the Phraohs? (A history of their names with a list of cartouches) Quirke, Stephen 1990 Dover Publications ISBN 0-486-26586-2 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Archives 
