0 @N6000000002187608465@ NOTE Gilukhipa
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Gilukhipa was daughter of Shuttarna II, king of Mitanni. For political reasons, she was sent to Egypt to join Amenhotep III in marriage. The Egyptian pharaoh made a special issue of commemorative scarabs on the occasion of his mar
1 CONC riage to Gilukhipa, where he recorded that the princess was escorted by 317 ladies-in-waiting, women from the Mitanni king's royal palace. Gilukhipa became known as the "Secondary King's Wife," meaning she was secondary to Amenhot
1 CONC ep III's other wife, Queen Tiye.
1 CONT
1 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Mitanni or Mittani (in Assyrian sources Hanilgalbat, Khanigalbat) was a kingdom in northern Syria. The name was later used as a geographical term for the area between the Khabur and Euphrates rivers in Neo-Assyrian times. Mitanni 
1 CONC was a feudal state led by a warrior nobility.
1 CONT
1 CONT Mitanni in northern Syria extended from Nuzi (modern Kirkuk) and the river Tigris in the east, to Aleppo and middle Syria (Nuhashshe) in the west. Its centre was in the Khabur valley, with two capitals: Taite and Washshukanni, cal
1 CONC led Taidu and Ushshukana respectively in Assyrian sources. (Vasu-khani would mean "mine of wealth" in Sanskrit, but cf. Luwian vasu- "good") The whole area allows agriculture without artificial irrigation; cattle, sheep and goats 
1 CONC were raised. It is very similar to Assyria in climate, and was settled by both indigenous Hurrian and Amoritic-speaking (Amurru) populations.
