0 @N6000000002187610402@ NOTE Senusret III
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Senusret III (also seen as Senwosret III or Sesostris III) was a pharaoh of Egypt. He ruled from 1878 BC to 1839 BC, and was the fifth monarch of the Twelfth Dynasty of the Middle Kingdom. He was a Great Pharaoh of the twelfth Dyn
1 CONC asty and is supposed to be the most powerful Egyptian ruler of this time. For this, he is regarded as one of the sources for the legend about Sesostris. Other forms of the name Senusret are Senwosret and Sesostris.
1 CONT
1 CONT Senusret III continued his Kingdom's expansion deep into Nubia (from 1866 to 1863 BC) where he erected massive River Forts including Buhen and Toshka at Uronarti. One stela mentions his military activities against both Nubia and P
1 CONC alestine [1]. Morgan, in 1894, reported rock inscriptions near Sehel Island documenting his digging of a canal [2]. He erected a temple and town in Abydos, and another temple in Medamud [3].
1 CONT
1 CONT His pyramid was constructed at Dahshur. A papyrus in the Berlin Museum shows Year 20 of his reign is equivalent to Year 1 of his son Amenemhat III. This means that he initiated a coregency with his son in this year. According to J
1 CONC osef Wegner, a Year 39 hieratic control note was recovered on a white limestone block from "a securely defined deposit of construction debris produced from the building of the Senwosret III mortuary temple. The fragment itself is 
1 CONC part of the remnants of the temple construction. This deposit provides evidence for the date of construction of the mortuary temple of Senwosret III at Abydos."(p.251) Wegner stresses that it is unlikely that Amenemhet III, Senusr
1 CONC et's son and successor would still be working on his father's temple nearly 4 decades into his own reign and notes that the only possible solution for the block's existence here is that Senusret III had a 39 Year reign, with the f
1 CONC inal 20 years in co-regency with Amenemhet III. Since the project was associated with a project of Senusret III, his Regnal Year was presumably used to date the block, rather than Year 20 of Amenemhet III. This implies that Senusr
1 CONC et was still alive in the first 2 decades of his son's reign prior to his death.
1 CONT
1 CONT Visually, Senusret III is known for his somber sculptures in which he appears careworn and grave.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * W. Grajetzki, The Middle Kingdom of Ancient Egypt: History,Archaeology and Society, Duckworth, London 2006 ISBN 0715634356, 51-58
1 CONT     * Josef Wegner, The Nature and Chronology of the Senwosret III-Amenemhat III Regnal Succession: Some Considerations based on new evidence from the Mortuary Temple of Senwosret III at Abydos", JNES 55, Vol.4, (1996), pp.249-279
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT External links
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Senusret (III) Khakhaure
1 CONT     * Senusret III: The fifth king of the 12th Dynasty
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Senusret II Pharaoh of Egypt
1 CONT 1878 – 1840 BC
1 CONT Twelfth Dynasty Succeeded by:
1 CONT Amenemhat III
1 CONT
1 CONT ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT conquered Nubia
1 CONT
1 CONT  Senusret III, 1878-1843 BCE, fixed Egypt's southern border above the second cataract of the Nile. He also waged campaigns aimed at combating the Libyans of the Western Desert and retaining Egyptian influence and trade ties with S
1 CONC yria and Canaan. He supervised the design and construction of numerous public works and curbed the power of the nobility. These efforts led to an ever greater centralisation of the administration and concentration of power in the 
1 CONC capital, with an accompanying growth of well-being, and a decline of the provinces. 
0 @N6000000002187610364@ NOTE Amenemhat III
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Amenemhat III (ca. 1860 BC-1797 BC) was a pharaoh of Egypt. He ruled from 1842 BC to 1797 BC, and is regarded as the greatest monarch of the Middle Kingdom. He may have had a long co-regency (of 20 years) with his father, Sesostri
1 CONC s III. He later instituted a coregency with his successor Amenemhet IV based upon a now damaged rock inscription at Konosso in Nubia which equates Year 1 of Amenemhet IV to either Year 46, 47 or 48 of his reign. His daughter Sobek
1 CONC neferu later succeded Amenemhat IV.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT He build a first pyramid at Dahshur (the so-called "Black Pyramid") but there were building problems and this was abandoned. Around year 15 of his reign the king decided to build a new pyramid at Hawara. The pyramid at Dahshur was
1 CONC  used as burial ground for several royal women.
1 CONT
1 CONT His mortuary temple at Hawara (near the Fayum), is accompanied by a pyramid and was known to Herodotus and Diodorus Siculus as the "Labyrinth." Strabo praised it as a wonder of the world. The king's pyramid at Hawara contained som
1 CONC e of the most complex security features of any found in Egypt and is perhaps the only one to come close to the sort of tricks Hollywood associates with such structures. Nevertheless, the king's burial was robbed in antiquity. His 
1 CONC daughter, Neferu-Ptah, was buried in a separate pyramid (discovered in 1956) 2km southwest of the king's.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Rhind Papyrus is thought to have been originally composed during Amenemhat's time. He is thought to have enjoyed a reign of between 45 to 47 Years although his Highest Year Date is a papyrus dated to Regnal Year 46, I Akhet 22
1 CONC  of his reign.
1 CONT
1 CONT Other names:
1 CONT
1 CONT Ni-Maat-Rê (throne name) 
1 CONT Aa-bau (Horus name) 
1 CONT Ammenemes 
1 CONT Lamares, Ameres (According to Manetho) 
1 CONT Moeris 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT External link
1 CONT Amenemhat (III) Nimaatre 
1 CONT   This ancient Egyptian article is a stub. You can help Wikipedia by expanding it. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Senusret III Pharaoh of Egypt
1 CONT Twelfth Dynasty Succeeded by:
1 CONT Amenemhat IV 
1 CONT
1 CONT ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Amenemhet III (Nimaatre) (1817-1772 BCE) completed the building of the great waterwheels of the Fayum, thus diverting the flood waters of the Nile into Lake Moeris. The irrigation system and an overflow canal, was used to drain th
1 CONC e marshes. An estimated 153,600 acres of fertile land was reclaimed from the water. 
1 CONT     Amenemhet raised two colossal statues of himself nearby to celebrate this feat. Among his many achievements was the famous Labyrinth, also known as the Pyramid of Hawara, one of the great wonders of the ancient world. The cent
1 CONC ral burial chamber of the pyramid, carved from a single block of granite, is estimated to have weighed 110 tons. His pyramidal tomb was built at Dashur, which he abandoned in favor of the Hawara Pyrami
1 CONT     Under Amenemhet copper was mined in the Sinai and local mines, often under dreadful conditions for the miners. 
1 CONT     According to two missives Amenemhet prevented a migration of starving Nubians into Upper Egypt by providing food aid, sending bread and beer to the drought stricken region. 
0 @N6000000002187610383@ NOTE Amenemhat IV
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Amenemhat IV was Pharaoh of Egypt, likely ruling between 1808 BC - 1799 BC. He served first as a junior coregent to Amenemhat III and completed the latter's temples at Medinet Maadi. He also likely also built a temple in the north
1 CONC eastern Fayum at Qasr el-Sagha.
1 CONT
1 CONT His short reign was relatively uneventful, but it was during this time that decline of the Middle Kingdom is generally thought to have begun.
1 CONT
1 CONT Amenemhet died without an eligible male heir, and was succeeded by his half-sister (or perhaps his aunt) Sobeknefru, who became the first woman in about 1500 years to rule Egypt. He may have been Sobeknefru's spouse but no histori
1 CONC cal evidence currently substantiates this idea. According to the Turin Canon papyrus, Amenemhet IV had a reign of 9 Years 3 Months and 27 days.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Amenemhat III Pharaoh of Egypt
1 CONT Twelfth Dynasty Succeeded by:
1 CONT Queen Sobeknefru 
0 @N6000000002187988882@ NOTE Amenemhat IV
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Amenemhat IV was Pharaoh of Egypt, likely ruling between 1815 BC - 1806 BC. He served first as a junior coregent to Amenemhat III and completed the latter's temple at Medinet Madi. The Temple of Medinet Madi is "the only intact te
1 CONC mple still existing from the Middle Kingdom" according to Zahi Hawass, Secretary-General of the Supreme Council of Antiquities (SCA).[1] The temple's foundations, administrative buildings, granaries and residences were recently un
1 CONC covered by an Egyptian archaeological expedition in early 2006. Amenemhat IV likely also built a temple in the northeastern Fayum at Qasr el-Sagha.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to the Turin Canon papyrus, Amenemhet IV enjoyed a reign of 9 Years 3 Months and 27 days. He served the first year of his reign as the junior co-regent to his powerful predecessor, Amenemhet III according to a rock graff
1 CONC ito in Nubia. His short reign was relatively peaceful and uneventful; several dated expeditions were recorded at the mines Serabit el-Khadim in the Sinai during his reign. It was shortly after his death that the gradual decline of
1 CONC  the Middle Kingdom is thought to have begun.
1 CONT
1 CONT Amenemhat died without an eligible male heir, and was succeeded by his half-sister (or perhaps his aunt) Sobeknefru, who became the first woman in about 1500 years to rule Egypt. He may have been Sobeknefru's spouse but no histori
1 CONC cal evidence currently substantiates this idea.
1 CONT
1 CONT Amenemhet IV may have been Moses.[citation needed]
1 CONT [edit]
