0 @N6000000002187604319@ NOTE Necho I
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Necho I (672 BC–664 BC) was Governor of the Egyptian city of Sais. His rule lasted 8 Years according to Manetho as a local Saite king of the Twenty-sixth dynasty of Egypt. Egypt was reunified by his son, Psamtik I. Necho is primar
1 CONC ily attested to in Assyrian documents but now also in a one contemporary native Egyptian document from his reign. He was officially "installed" at Sais by Assurbanipal around 670 BC, but he already ruled Egypt as a local king prio
1 CONC r this event. According to historical records, Necho I was killed by an invading Kushite force in 664 BC under Tantamani for being an ally of Assyria. The Nubian invasion into the Egyptian Delta was subsequently repelled by the As
1 CONC syrians who proceeded to advance south into Upper Egypt and sack Thebes.
1 CONT
1 CONT Necho I's Year 2 is now attested on a privately held donation stela that was first discovered and published by Olivier Perdu in a 2002 issue of CRAIBL or 'Compte-rendus de l'Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres, Paris.' The
1 CONC  stela records a large land donation to the Osirian triad of PerHebyt (modern Behbeit el-Hagar near Sebennytos) by the "priest of Isis, Mistress of Hebyt, Great Chief...son of Iuput, Akanosh." The stela is very similiar in style, 
1 CONC content and epigraphy with the Year 8 stela of Shepsesre Tefnakht. This suggests that Shepsesre Tefnakht was Tefnakht II who was different from the famous Great Chief of the West Tefnakhte who opposed Piye. Shepsesre would, hence,
1 CONC  have been a relatively recent predecessor of Necho I and be the "Stephinates" of Manetho's records. Perdu convincingly argues that the 2 prior kings Stephinates and Nekauba(or Nechepso) in Manetho's Epitome belong to the start of
1 CONC  the 26th Saite Dynasty.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT External references
1 CONT Olivier Perdu, "De Stéphinatès à Néchao ou les débuts de la XXVIe dynastie"(From Tefnakht II to Necho and the start of the 26th Dynasty), CRAIBL 2002, pp. 1215-1244 
0 @N6000000002187604180@ NOTE Psammetichus I
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Psammetichus, or Psamtik I, was the first of three kings of the Saite, or Twenty-sixth dynasty of Egypt (664 BC-610 BC). The name is frankly written so as to mean "the man of methek", or mixed drink, implying "heavy drinker" or pe
1 CONC rhaps "vendor of strong drink". The Egyptian scribes do not conceal the opprobrious elements, but it has been suggested that the name may be due to false etymology of a foreign name (though all the names throughout the dynasty app
1 CONC ear to be Egyptian), or that Methek may have been an unknown deity.
1 CONT
1 CONT The story in Herodotus of the Dodecarchy and the rise of Psamtik is fanciful. It is known from cuneiform texts that twenty local princelings were appointed by Esarhaddon and confirmed by Assurbanipal to govern Egypt. Necho I, fath
1 CONC er of Psammetichus, was the chief of these kinglets, but they seem to have been quite unable to hold the Egyptians to the hated Assyrians against the more sympathetic Nubians. The labyrinth built by a king of the Twelfth dynasty o
1 CONC f Egypt is ascribed by Herodotus to the Dodecarchy, or rule of 12, which must represent this combination of rulers. If the dynasties were numbered thus before Manetho, the numeral may be the cause of Herodotus's confusion. After h
1 CONC is father's death, Psammetichus was able to defy the Assyrians and the Nubians, and during a long reign marked by intimate relations with the Greeks, restored the prosperity of Egypt.
1 CONT
1 CONT Psamtik I effectively reunified Egypt in his 8th Year(656 BC) when his naval fleet sailed into Upper Egypt and compelled the existing God's Wife of Amun at Thebes to adopt his daughter Nitocris as her Heiress in the so-called Adop
1 CONC tion Stela. Psamtik's success destroyed the last vestiges of the Nubian Dynasty's control over Upper Egypt under Tantamani since Thebes now accepted his authority. Nitocris would come to serve in office from 656 BC until her death
1 CONC  in 586 BC. Psamtik I himself was a great Pharaoh of Egypt who won Egypt's independence from the Assyrian Empire. He also encouraged many Greek colonies to settle in Egypt and serve in the services of his state.
1 CONT
1 CONT Herodotus, the historian, wrote that Psammetichus (Psamtik) sought to discover the origin of language by conducting an experiment with two children. Allegedly he gave two newborn babies to a shepherd, with the instructions that no
1 CONC  one should speak to them, but that the shepherd should feed and care for them while listening to determine their first words. The hypothesis was that the first word would be uttered in the root language of all people. When one of
1 CONC  the children cried “becos” with outstretched arms the shepherd concluded that the word was Phrygian because that was the sound of Phyrgian word for “bread.” Thus, they concluded that the Phyrgian were an older people than the Egy
1 CONC ptians. Whether or not the Herodotus story is true is not known. However the example of an experiment in language represents at least an interest in the age of humanity in antiquity, and a simple (although less than ethical from a
1 CONC  modern view) experiment.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Necho I Pharaoh of Egypt
1 CONT 664 – 610 BC
1 CONT Twenty-sixth Dynasty Succeeded by:
1 CONT Necho II 
