0 @N6000000002187604091@ NOTE Psammetichus II
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Psammetichus II (also spelled Psammeticus, Psammetich, and Psamtik II) was a king of the Twenty-sixth dynasty of Egypt (595 BC-589 BC).
1 CONT
1 CONT Psammetichus marched into the Kingdom of Judah, Philistia, and Phoenicia in about 592 BC in response to moves made by Babylon, and attempted to generate anti-Babylonian sentiment among their leaders. Wahibre (Apries) succeeded him
1 CONC .
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Necho II Pharaoh of Egypt
1 CONT 595 – 589 BC
1 CONT Twenty-sixth Dynasty Succeeded by:
1 CONT Apries
0 @N6000000000437045119@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> Of Egypt
0 @N6000000000437386964@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> Of Egypt
0 @N6000000002187604062@ NOTE Apries
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Apries (?p????) is the name by which Herodotus (ii. 161) and Diodorus (i. 68) designate Wahibre, ??af??? (Pharaoh-Hophra), a pharaoh of Egypt (589 BC-570 BC), the fourth king (counting from Psammetichus I) of the Twenty-sixth dyna
1 CONC sty of Egypt. He was equated with the Waphres of Manetho, who records he reigned 19 years. He is called Hophra in Jeremiah 44:30.
1 CONT
1 CONT Apries inherited the throne from his father, the undistingished Psammetichus II and continued his poor military record. Unsuccessful attempts to intervene in the kingdom of Judah were followed by a mutiny of soldiers at Aswan. An 
1 CONC attempt to protect Libya from incursions by Greek forces was also unsuccessful and the returning troops squabbled with the existing order. Apries was killed in a conflict with his eventual successor Amasis II, a former general who
1 CONC  had declared himself pharaoh.
1 CONT
1 CONT Eusebius placed the eclipse of Thales in 585 BC in the eighth or twelfth year of his reign.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Psammetichus II Pharaoh of Egypt
1 CONT Twenty-sixth Dynasty Succeeded by:
1 CONT Amasis II 
